0":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-79","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#module-map","content":"`train/*.py`, `train/dataset/*.py`, `tools/multispeaker.py`","locale":"en"},"9091":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-80","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#runtime","content":"`Runtime` in `engine/runtime.py` holds the device, dtype, CUDA Graph switches, chunk configuration and loaded model caches. The server process has one (built by core's `ComputeService`); the Live worker and each training or separation subprocess build their own, so their GPU memory is independent.","locale":"en"},"9092":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-81","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#gpu-rules-and-device-selection","content":"The original executes GPU rules and parses `argparse` arguments when `configs/config.py` is imported (the CLI and VST worker must therefore clear `sys.argv` first). Here it is a pure function, `choose_device(requested, precision) -> DeviceChoice`:","locale":"en"},"9093":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-82","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#gpu-rules-and-device-selection","content":"Condition (`cuda_profile`)","locale":"en"},"9094":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-83","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#gpu-rules-and-device-selection","content":"Result","locale":"en"},"9095":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-84","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#gpu-rules-and-device-selection","content":"GPU memory \\< 4 GiB or compute capability \\< 5.3","locale":"en"},"9096":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-85","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#gpu-rules-and-device-selection","content":"Ineligible (`eligible=False`)","locale":"en"},"9097":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-86","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#gpu-rules-and-device-selection","content":"Compute capability 6.1 (Pascal), or name contains `16xx` with compute capability 7.5 (GTX 16 series)","locale":"en"},"9098":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-87","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#gpu-rules-and-device-selection","content":"fp32","locale":"en"},"9099":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-88","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#gpu-rules-and-device-selection","content":"Compute capability > 6.1","locale":"en"},"9100":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-89","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#gpu-rules-and-device-selection","content":"fp16","locale":"en"},"9101":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-90","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#gpu-rules-and-device-selection","content":"Otherwise","locale":"en"},"9102":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-91","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#gpu-rules-and-device-selection","content":"Ineligible","locale":"en"},"9103":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-92","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#gpu-rules-and-device-selection","content":"GPU memory is calculated as `total_memory / 1024³ + 0.4`, matching the original. With `requested=\"auto\"`, it selects the eligible CUDA card with the greatest `(sm, memory_gb)`; if none, it tries DirectML (`directml_device()` actually runs a tensor operation to confirm it works), then falls back to CPU. An explicit `cuda:N` is used even if the rules would skip it, but only in fp32; unavailable `dml` or `mps` falls back to CPU with an explanation in `reason`. `precision` set to `fp32`/`fp16` overrides the rules (fp16 applies only to CUDA and XPU). MPS is used only when explicitly requested; `auto` never selects it.","locale":"en"},"9104":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-93","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#gpu-rules-and-device-selection","content":"On a ROCm build (`is_rocm()`: `torch.version.hip`), AMD cards are `cuda:N` and `cuda_profile` skips the SM rows (the capability is the gfx version): at least 4 GiB, fp16. Intel cards are `xpu:N` (`xpu_profile`: at least 4 GiB, fp16 when `has_fp16`). One torch build carries one of the two, so `gpu_backend()` (`cuda` or `xpu`) and `gpu_profiles()` give the devices that training's integer GPU ids refer to; `visible_devices_env()` hides the others from a child (`CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES`, read by HIP too, or `ZE_AFFINITY_MASK`). Training's autocast and `GradScaler` take `xpu` there, and DDP uses `xccl` instead of `gloo`. `configure_cuda_graph` never turns graphs on under ROCm. pymss knows no XPU, so separation loads on the CPU, moves the net and stays fp32.","locale":"en"},"9105":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-94","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#gpu-rules-and-device-selection","content":"`list_devices()` lists all devices (CPU last), letting `GET /compute/devices` and `rvc-next doctor` show the rule verdict for each card.","locale":"en"},"9106":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-95","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"`ChunkConfig.for_device()` gives the original's `x_pad/x_query/x_center/x_max` (seconds):","locale":"en"},"9107":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-96","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"Device","locale":"en"},"9108":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-97","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"`x_pad`","locale":"en"},"9109":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-98","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"`x_query`","locale":"en"},"9110":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-99","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"`x_center`","locale":"en"},"9111":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-100","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"`x_max`","locale":"en"},"9112":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-101","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"CUDA with GPU memory ≤ 4 GiB","locale":"en"},"9113":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-102","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"1","locale":"en"},"9114":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-103","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"5","locale":"en"},"9115":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-104","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"30","locale":"en"},"9116":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-105","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"32","locale":"en"},"9117":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-106","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"fp16","locale":"en"},"9118":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-107","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"3","locale":"en"},"9119":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-108","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"10","locale":"en"},"9120":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-109","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"60","locale":"en"},"9121":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-110","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"65","locale":"en"},"9122":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-111","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"Otherwise (fp32, CPU)","locale":"en"},"9123":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-112","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"1","locale":"en"},"9124":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-113","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"6","locale":"en"},"9125":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-114","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"38","locale":"en"},"9126":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-115","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"41","locale":"en"},"9127":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-116","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#chunk-configuration","content":"Golden tests run on CPU fp32, and the original's side uses the last row (`_OriginalConfig` in `scripts/golden.py`).","locale":"en"},"9128":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-117","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-caches","content":"Method","locale":"en"},"9129":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-118","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-caches","content":"Cache key","locale":"en"},"9130":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-119","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-caches","content":"Policy","locale":"en"},"9131":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-120","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-caches","content":"`hubert()`","locale":"en"},"9132":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-121","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-caches","content":"Single entry","locale":"en"},"9133":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-122","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-caches","content":"Load once","locale":"en"},"9134":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-123","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-caches","content":"`f0(method)`","locale":"en"},"9135":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-124","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-caches","content":"`engine.f0.METHODS`","locale":"en"},"9136":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-125","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-caches","content":"Load each once (`engine/f0/__init__.py:create_provider`)","locale":"en"},"9137":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-126","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-caches","content":"`voice(path)`","locale":"en"},"9138":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-127","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-caches","content":"Resolved path + `st_mtime_ns`","locale":"en"},"9139":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-128","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-caches","content":"LRU, retains the last `max_voices` (default 3); eviction clears the network's CUDA Graph cache","locale":"en"},"9140":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-129","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-caches","content":"`index(path)`","locale":"en"},"9141":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-130","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-caches","content":"Path + `st_mtime_ns`","locale":"en"},"9142":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-131","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-caches","content":"Load each file once","locale":"en"},"9143":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-132","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-caches","content":"Including modification time in the key makes overwriting a file (re-exporting, rebuilding an index) take effect automatically, without notifying the runtime. All methods share an `RLock` and can be called from multiple threads.","locale":"en"},"9144":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-133","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-caches","content":"`cached()` returns names such as `hubert`, `f0:<method>`, `voice:<filename>`, `index:<filename>` for the compute panel; `release()` unloads everything and calls `soft_empty_cache()`; `release_idle(seconds)` unloads everything when all entries have been idle longer than the given duration (called by core according to `compute.unload_after_minutes`); on CUDA, `vram()` uses `torch.cuda.mem_get_info` to return `(used, total)` MiB.","locale":"en"},"9145":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-134","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#gpu-memory-release-and-oom","content":"These two functions follow ComfyUI's `comfy/model_management.py`:","locale":"en"},"9146":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-135","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#gpu-memory-release-and-oom","content":"`soft_empty_cache()`: `gc.collect()` first, then on CUDA `synchronize` → `empty_cache` → `ipc_collect` (`torch.mps.empty_cache()` on MPS). Synchronising first ensures no kernel is still using the blocks being returned. Does nothing if torch is not loaded.","locale":"en"},"9147":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-136","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#gpu-memory-release-and-oom","content":"`is_oom(error)`: identifies `torch.OutOfMemoryError`, CUDA `AcceleratorError` with code 2, and `RuntimeError`/`MemoryError` messages containing \"out of memory\"/\"not enough gpu video memory\" (MPS, DirectML). When it detects an asynchronous CUDA error, it triggers a small operation and synchronises to consume the pending error so the next operation does not inherit it. It gets torch from `sys.modules`, without importing torch just to classify an error.","locale":"en"},"9148":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-137","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#gpu-memory-release-and-oom","content":"Who unloads what after OOM is a core policy (`release_after_oom`); see Core services.","locale":"en"},"9149":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-138","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#cuda-graph","content":"`engine/graph.py` copies the original's `tools/cuda_graph.py`: each “owner” object (`net_g`, HuBERT, RMVPE, resampler…) carries a `_GraphCache`, keyed by call-site namespace plus each input tensor's shape, strides, dtype, device and `requires_grad`. A new signature is warmed up three times on a side stream, then captured; replay copies inputs into static tensors, calls `replay()` and clones outputs. Signatures that fail capture are recorded in `failures` and use eager execution thereafter. The cache evicts by LRU, with capacity from `RVC_NEXT_CUDA_GRAPH_MAX_CACHE` (default 8). `get_cuda_graph_stats()` returns capture, replay, fallback and eviction counts.","locale":"en"},"9150":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-139","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#cuda-graph","content":"The only difference from the original is the switch: the original reads `RVC_CUDA_GRAPH`; here `Runtime` calls `configure_cuda_graph(device, wanted)` at construction to set a process-wide switch, enabled only after the device passes a capture–replay probe (`detect_cuda_graph_support`). `wanted` comes from settings: the server uses `compute.cuda_graph_offline` (off by default, matching the original's offline default), and the Live worker uses `compute.cuda_graph_live` (on by default). Training never enables it.","locale":"en"},"9151":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-140","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#cuda-graph","content":"Callers uniformly use `run_cuda_graph(owner, namespace, fn, *inputs)`; when disabled or inputs are not on CUDA, it calls `fn` directly. Since shape is part of the key, namespaces must include parameters affecting shape (such as streaming's `rvc-realtime-f0-{skip}-{ret}-{ret2}` and offline formant's `rvc-synth-f0-formant-{n_res}`), or the same graph could be reused incorrectly.","locale":"en"},"9152":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-141","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#cuda-graph","content":"The offline converter's `_empty_cache()` calls `torch.cuda.empty_cache()` only when CUDA Graph is disabled: captured graphs' memory pools must be retained, or replay fails. This matches the original's `Pipeline`.","locale":"en"},"9153":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-142","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hubert-features","content":"`features/hubert.py` uses transformers' `HubertModel` plus a `final_proj` layer (`HubertModelWithFinalProj`), loaded with `local_files_only=True` from the asset directory `hubert_base/` (`config.json`, `preprocessor_config.json`, `pytorch_model.bin` or `model.safetensors`); missing files raise `MissingAssetError([\"hubert\"])`. DirectML (device string contains `privateuseone`) forces `attn_implementation=\"eager\"`, because DirectML does not implement all SDPA kernels used by transformers.","locale":"en"},"9154":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-143","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hubert-features","content":"`extract_features(model, source, version, padding_mask)`:","locale":"en"},"9155":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-144","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hubert-features","content":"v1: `hidden_states[9]` through `final_proj`, 256 dimensions;","locale":"en"},"9156":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-145","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hubert-features","content":"v2: `last_hidden_state`, 768 dimensions;","locale":"en"},"9157":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-146","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hubert-features","content":"A separate CUDA Graph namespace for each of the four combinations (version × mask present/absent).","locale":"en"},"9158":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-147","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hubert-features","content":"**Normalisation has one implementation**: `normalize_input()` reads `do_normalize` from `preprocessor_config.json` (`requires_normalization()`, `lru_cache`) and applies `F.layer_norm` if true. The original follows this flag in training feature extraction but never normalises in inference, potentially making training and inference inconsistent; the real asset's `do_normalize` is `false`, so neither side normalises and results match the original. Training (`train/feature_extract.py`) and inference share this function, preventing divergence if the asset changes later.","locale":"en"},"9159":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-148","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"`f0/base.py` defines the `F0Provider` protocol: `compute(x, p_len)` returns a Hz value every 10 ms (160 samples) for 16 kHz audio, with 0 for unvoiced frames. The original implements pitch shift, unvoiced interpolation and 256-level quantisation separately in the offline pipeline, live engine and training extraction; here each has one implementation:","locale":"en"},"9160":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-149","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"`interpolate_unvoiced()`: fills unvoiced frames by linear interpolation between adjacent voiced frames (offline rule);","locale":"en"},"9161":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-150","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"`shift(f0, semitones)`: multiplies by `2^(semitones/12)`;","locale":"en"},"9162":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-151","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"`to_coarse()`: quantises on the mel scale to 1..255 for the synthesizer's pitch embeddings;","locale":"en"},"9163":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-152","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"`offline_f0()`: compute → interpolate → shift → quantise, returning `(coarse, hz)`, equivalent to the original's `Pipeline.get_f0`.","locale":"en"},"9164":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-153","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"The streaming engine has its own `_get_f0`/`_f0_post` to handle tensors on the device, with the same maths (see below). Training F0 is not pitch-shifted.","locale":"en"},"9165":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-154","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"Method","locale":"en"},"9166":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-155","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"Implementation","locale":"en"},"9167":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-156","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"Device","locale":"en"},"9168":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-157","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"Asset","locale":"en"},"9169":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-158","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"`pm`","locale":"en"},"9170":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-159","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"`PmProvider`: parselmouth `to_pitch_ac`, 10 ms time step, voicing threshold 0.6, 50–1100 Hz, zero-pad both ends to `p_len`","locale":"en"},"9171":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-160","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"CPU","locale":"en"},"9172":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-161","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"None","locale":"en"},"9173":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-162","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"`rmvpe`","locale":"en"},"9174":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-163","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"`RmvpeProvider` → `f0/rmvpe.py:RMVPE` (copied), `infer_from_audio(x, thred=0.03)`","locale":"en"},"9175":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-164","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"PyTorch on CUDA/CPU; onnxruntime's `DmlExecutionProvider` on DirectML","locale":"en"},"9176":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-165","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"`rmvpe` (`rmvpe/rmvpe.pt`); `rmvpe-onnx` (`rmvpe/rmvpe.onnx`) on DirectML","locale":"en"},"9177":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-166","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"`fcpe`","locale":"en"},"9178":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-167","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"`FcpeProvider` → `f0/fcpe.py:FCPEInfer` → `f0/fcpe_model.py`, `local_argmax` decoding, threshold 0.006","locale":"en"},"9179":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-168","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"All; mel and decoding on CPU for DirectML","locale":"en"},"9180":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-169","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"`fcpe` (`fcpe/fcpe_c_v001.pt`)","locale":"en"},"9181":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-170","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"`crepe`, `crepe-tiny`","locale":"en"},"9182":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-171","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"`CrepeProvider` → `f0/crepe_model.py:crepe_f0`: Viterbi decoding, periodicity median-filtered over 3 frames, f0 mean-filtered over 3, 0 below periodicity 0.1, 50–1100 Hz (RVC's and Applio's torchcrepe call)","locale":"en"},"9183":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-172","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"All; CPU on DirectML","locale":"en"},"9184":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-173","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"`crepe` (`crepe/full.pth`), `crepe-tiny` (`crepe/tiny.pth`)","locale":"en"},"9185":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-174","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"`swift`","locale":"en"},"9186":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-175","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"`SwiftProvider` → `f0/swift_model.py` (SwiftF0 0.3.0), Applio's subharmonic repair, 16 ms frames interpolated in log frequency to 10 ms, voiced at confidence ≥ 0.5","locale":"en"},"9187":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-176","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"All; CPU on DirectML","locale":"en"},"9188":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-177","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"None: `f0/data/swift_f0.pt` ships in the package","locale":"en"},"9189":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-178","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#f0-extraction","content":"`RmvpeProvider` checks the appropriate file for the device and raises `MissingAssetError` with the correct asset id if missing, so the UI offers ONNX or PyTorch weights as appropriate. `RMVPE` uses half precision only on CUDA (already decided by the runtime's GPU rules).","locale":"en"},"9190":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-179","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#the-fcpe-port","content":"The original uses FCPE through `torchfcpe.spawn_bundled_infer_model`. `f0/fcpe_model.py` ports only the path this model actually executes: `Wav2Mel` (128-bin log-mel, 16 kHz, hop 160), convolution-only `CFNaiveMelPE` (`ConvOnlyEncoder` stacking `ConformerConvModule`), two decoders and `load_fcpe()`. The attention branch (depending on `einops`, `local_attention`) never runs for `fcpe_c_v001.pt` with `conv_only: true`, so it was not ported; non-16 kHz input is resampled by the ported `sinc_resample.Resample`, matching torchfcpe's use of torchaudio. Arithmetic is unchanged; output is bit-identical to torchfcpe (`tests/engine/test_fcpe_model.py`). This removes rvc-next's dependency on torchfcpe (which pulls in torchaudio), with weights downloaded as the `fcpe` asset (see Assets and model repositories for sources).","locale":"en"},"9191":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-180","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#the-fcpe-port","content":"`f0/fcpe.py:FCPEInfer` handles device differences on top of this: on CUDA with CUDA Graph enabled, mel is computed outside the graph, with the network and decoding inside (decoder offset tensors are placed on the device in advance because graph capture forbids host-to-device copies); DirectML lacks the complex STFT used in the mel stage, and `gather` returns incorrect bins, so mel and decoding run on CPU and only the network runs on GPU.","locale":"en"},"9192":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-181","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#retrieval-indexes","content":"**Retrieval** (`index/retrieval.py`): `load_index()` reads the faiss index and reconstructs all vectors once with `reconstruct_n`, producing `LoadedIndex(path, index, vectors)`; if the name contains `trained` and a corresponding `added` file exists beside it, it uses the latter (original behaviour). `blend(feats, index, k=8)` averages the top-8 neighbours weighted by inverse squared distance. The original repeats `read_index` + `reconstruct_n` for every converted file, slowing batch conversion; here `Runtime.index()` caches by path and modification time.","locale":"en"},"9193":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-182","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#retrieval-indexes","content":"**Identification** (`index/inspect.py`): `inspect_index()` opens with mmap and reads only `d` and `ntotal`. `IndexInfo.version` follows the dimension (256 → v1, 768 → v2); `empty` means `ntotal == 0`, a `trained_*` index without added vectors. Import sessions and the index inbox use this to check voice compatibility by content rather than filename.","locale":"en"},"9194":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-183","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#retrieval-indexes","content":"**Building** (`index/build.py`, training's `index` stage, ported from `train/train_index.py`): concatenate features, shuffle, save `total_fea*.npy`; above `KMEANS_THRESHOLD` (200,000) vectors, reduce to `KMEANS_CENTRES` (10,000) centres with faiss k-means (the original uses scikit-learn's MiniBatchKMeans; switching to faiss removes a dependency); `IVF{n},Flat`, `n = min(16·√N, N/39)`, `nprobe = 1`; write `trained_IVF…index`, then add vectors in batches of 8192 and write `added_IVF…index`. Multi-speaker experiments (with `multispeaker_manifest.json`) have one index per speaker, with `_spkid<N>` in the filename.","locale":"en"},"9195":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-184","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#retrieval-indexes","content":"The original skips building whenever any `added_*.index` exists, leaving an old index after training on new data. Here the fingerprint of each feature file group (`train/fingerprint.py:of_paths`, including version and name) is stored in `index_fingerprint.json`; old files are removed and rebuilt only if the fingerprint changes or `force=True`, otherwise it returns `reused: true`.","locale":"en"},"9196":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-185","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#networks","content":"`models/{synthesizer,modules,attentions,commons,transforms}.py` is copied from the original's `infer/module/`, changing only import paths and retaining the original formatting (excluded from `ruff format` and ty; see Parity with the original). Four synthesizer classes are selected by `(version, pitch_guidance)` (`models/loader.py:synthesizer_class`):","locale":"en"},"9197":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-186","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#networks","content":"With pitch guidance","locale":"en"},"9198":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-187","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#networks","content":"Without pitch guidance","locale":"en"},"9199":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-188","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#networks","content":"v1 (256 dimensions)","locale":"en"},"9200":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-189","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#networks","content":"`SynthesizerTrnMs256NSFsid`","locale":"en"},"9201":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-190","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#networks","content":"`SynthesizerTrnMs256NSFsid_nono`","locale":"en"},"9202":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-191","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#networks","content":"v2 (768 dimensions)","locale":"en"},"9203":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-192","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#networks","content":"`SynthesizerTrnMs768NSFsid`","locale":"en"},"9204":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-193","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#networks","content":"`SynthesizerTrnMs768NSFsid_nono`","locale":"en"},"9205":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-194","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#networks","content":"Discriminators are `MultiPeriodDiscriminator` (v1) and `MultiPeriodDiscriminatorV2` (v2).","locale":"en"},"9206":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-195","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"`models/checkpoint.py` determines whether users' existing models remain usable, so it reads and writes the original format:","locale":"en"},"9207":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-196","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"**Small model** (voice): `{weight: fp16 state_dict without enc_q, config: 18 positional hyperparameters, info, sr, f0, version, speaker_info?}`. `read_small_model()` parses it into `SmallModel`, retaining unknown keys in `extra` and preserving them when writing back; `target_sample_rate` comes from `config[-1]`, `speaker_slots` from the row count of `emb_g.weight`. `normalize_speaker_info()` normalises `speaker_info` into a list of `{id, name}` with unique ids below 110.","locale":"en"},"9208":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-197","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"**Full checkpoint**: `G_<step>.pth`/`D_<step>.pth`, `{model, optimizer, learning_rate, iteration}`; “Keep only the latest checkpoint” fixes the step at `2333333`.","locale":"en"},"9209":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-198","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"All writes use `torch_save()`, writing `.tmp` then `os.replace`, so a crash cannot leave a partial model.","locale":"en"},"9210":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-199","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"`summarize()` describes a small model or G checkpoint without constructing a network; `inspect_checkpoint()` identifies any `.pth` by content for import sessions, never raising for a readable file:","locale":"en"},"9211":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-200","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"Kind","locale":"en"},"9212":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-201","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"Identification","locale":"en"},"9213":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-202","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"`small`","locale":"en"},"9214":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-203","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"Has `weight` and `config`","locale":"en"},"9215":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-204","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"`D`","locale":"en"},"9216":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-205","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"State dict has `discriminators.*`; 7 sub-discriminators means v1, 9 means v2; D cannot identify the sample rate","locale":"en"},"9217":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-206","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"`G`","locale":"en"},"9218":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-207","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"Has `dec.*`; `enc_p.emb_phone.weight` width 768 means v2, otherwise v1; presence of `dec.m_source*` means pitch guidance; sample rate is looked up in `_G_RATES` from the input width of `enc_q.pre.weight` (513 → 32k, 1025 → 40k/48k) and upsampling kernel sizes","locale":"en"},"9219":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-208","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"`unknown`","locale":"en"},"9220":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-209","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"Anything else, with an explanation","locale":"en"},"9221":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-210","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"`enc_q.pre` width","locale":"en"},"9222":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-211","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"Upsampling kernels","locale":"en"},"9223":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-212","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"Result","locale":"en"},"9224":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-213","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"513","locale":"en"},"9225":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-214","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"`(16, 16, 4, 4, 4)`","locale":"en"},"9226":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-215","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"32k v1","locale":"en"},"9227":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-216","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"513","locale":"en"},"9228":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-217","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"`(20, 16, 4, 4)`","locale":"en"},"9229":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-218","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"32k v2","locale":"en"},"9230":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-219","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"1025","locale":"en"},"9231":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-220","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"`(16, 16, 4, 4)`","locale":"en"},"9232":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-221","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"40k (same architecture for v1 and v2)","locale":"en"},"9233":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-222","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"1025","locale":"en"},"9234":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-223","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"`(16, 16, 4, 4, 4)`","locale":"en"},"9235":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-224","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"48k v1","locale":"en"},"9236":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-225","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"1025","locale":"en"},"9237":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-226","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"`(24, 20, 4, 4)`","locale":"en"},"9238":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-227","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#pth-format","content":"48k v2","locale":"en"},"9239":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-228","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loading-merging-extraction-and-metadata-edits","content":"`loader.py:load_voice()`: reads the small model, changes `config[-3]` (speaker count) to the actual row count of `emb_g`, constructs the appropriate synthesizer, deletes `enc_q`, calls `load_state_dict(strict=False)`, then converts to half or single precision as selected. Equivalent to the original's `VC.get_vc`, without returning Gradio update dictionaries.","locale":"en"},"9240":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-229","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loading-merging-extraction-and-metadata-edits","content":"`merge.py:merge_models(a, b, dst, alpha=…)`: `alpha·a + (1−alpha)·b`, with fp16 results. Both must have the same version, pitch guidance, sample rate and structure; differing `emb_g` row counts keep the shorter one (original rule); sample rate and version come from `a`; `speaker_info` is retained only if identical in both.","locale":"en"},"9241":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-230","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loading-merging-extraction-and-metadata-edits","content":"`extract.py:extract_small_model()`: extracts a small model from a G checkpoint. `config_for(sample_rate, version)` generates the 18 configuration entries written by the original—v2 40k uses the v1 40k layout, matching the original's `configs/v1/40k.json`.","locale":"en"},"9242":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-231","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loading-merging-extraction-and-metadata-edits","content":"`checkpoint.py:change_info()`: copies a small model and rewrites `info` or speaker names (the original's “change information”). The voice library stores user-edited speaker names in `voice.json`, without modifying `.pth`.","locale":"en"},"9243":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-232","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#offline-conversion","content":"`OfflineConverter.convert(audio_16k, voice, params, index=…, resample_to=…, progress=…, cancel=…)` in `convert/offline.py` ports the original's `infer/vc/pipeline.py` line by line and returns `(int16 samples, sample rate)`. Input is float32 already decoded to 16 kHz mono (core handles decoding through `audio/io.py:decode`).","locale":"en"},"9244":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-233","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#offline-conversion","content":"Key points:","locale":"en"},"9245":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-234","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#offline-conversion","content":"**F0 is computed once** for the entire padded audio, then sliced per chunk to avoid pitch discontinuities at chunk boundaries.","locale":"en"},"9246":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-235","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#offline-conversion","content":"**Index**: retrieval is skipped entirely when `index_rate == 0` or there is no index. Blending is in fp32 (convert half precision to fp32, then back), matching the original.","locale":"en"},"9247":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-236","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#offline-conversion","content":"**protect**: when `protect < 0.5` and pitch guidance is present, original features are mixed back according to `pitchf`—weight 1 for voiced frames, `protect` for unvoiced frames—to protect consonants and breaths. `0.5` disables protection. The 2026 package interpolates unvoiced frames before this test, so with `unvoiced = \"original\"` no frame is below 1 Hz and protect never applies; `\"protect\"` (the core's default) takes the mask from the detector's own voicing (`offline_f0` returns it) and still feeds the interpolated pitch, and `\"zero\"` keeps 0 Hz there, as classic RVC. `StreamEngine` applies the same blend when `unvoiced` is not `\"original\"`, from a voicing cache shifted with `cache_pitchf` (the original has no protect in realtime).","locale":"en"},"9248":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-237","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#offline-conversion","content":"**Speaker** `params.speaker_id` is passed directly to the synthesizer as `sid`.","locale":"en"},"9249":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-238","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#offline-conversion","content":"**Cancellation and progress**: `cancel.check()` before each chunk, `progress(n/total)` after each chunk.","locale":"en"},"9250":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-239","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#offline-formant-shift","content":"The original supports formant shift only in the live engine; here its live method is ported to offline conversion:","locale":"en"},"9251":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-240","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#offline-formant-shift","content":"Shift F0 by `pitch − formant` (same as live);","locale":"en"},"9252":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-241","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#offline-formant-shift","content":"`factor = 2^(formant/12)`: request `n_res = ceil(frames · factor)` frames from the synthesizer and multiply NSF's continuous pitch `pitchf` by `n_res / frames`, preserving pitch while generating longer audio;","locale":"en"},"9253":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-242","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#offline-formant-shift","content":"`_formant_resample()` takes `upp_res = floor(factor · tgt_sr / 100)` samples per frame, then compresses back to the original length with `sinc_resample.Resample(upp_res → tgt_sr/100)`—restoring duration while shifting the spectral envelope upward by `factor`.","locale":"en"},"9254":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-243","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#offline-formant-shift","content":"When `formant == 0`, `n_res` is `None` and the call is exactly the same as the original, so output with default parameters is bit-identical.","locale":"en"},"9255":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-244","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#offline-formant-shift","content":"Output may be up to two 10 ms frames shorter than input, due to the original's per-chunk flooring in `p_len = len // 160`, deliberately preserved for bit-identical results.","locale":"en"},"9256":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-245","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#streaming-engine","content":"`StreamEngine` in `stream/engine.py` is a live converter with “one block in, one block out, no I/O”. Its maths comes from the original's `infer/rtrvc.py` (`RVC.infer`) and `start_vc`, `audio_callback` in `realtime_gui.py`; the original has three copies of this DSP chain in the GUI callback, `rtrvc` and VST worker, now combined in one class. `process()` is synchronous and accepts only plain arrays, so it can be driven by a PortAudio session, tests using WAV files, or a future VST adapter or browser transport.","locale":"en"},"9257":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-246","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#buffers-_build","content":"All lengths use the device sample rate `sample_rate`, with `zc = sample_rate // 100` (10 ms); millisecond parameters are rounded to whole 10 ms units (`_frames`, the original's rounding):","locale":"en"},"9258":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-247","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#buffers-_build","content":"Quantity","locale":"en"},"9259":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-248","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#buffers-_build","content":"Value","locale":"en"},"9260":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-249","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#buffers-_build","content":"`block_frame`","locale":"en"},"9261":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-250","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#buffers-_build","content":"`block_ms`","locale":"en"},"9262":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-251","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#buffers-_build","content":"`crossfade_frame`","locale":"en"},"9263":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-252","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#buffers-_build","content":"`crossfade_ms`","locale":"en"},"9264":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-253","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#buffers-_build","content":"`sola_buffer_frame`","locale":"en"},"9265":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-254","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#buffers-_build","content":"`min(crossfade_frame, 4·zc)` (at most 40 ms)","locale":"en"},"9266":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-255","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#buffers-_build","content":"`sola_search_frame`","locale":"en"},"9267":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-256","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#buffers-_build","content":"`zc` (10 ms)","locale":"en"},"9268":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-257","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#buffers-_build","content":"`extra_frame`","locale":"en"},"9269":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-258","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#buffers-_build","content":"`context_ms` (the original's `extra_time`)","locale":"en"},"9270":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-259","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#buffers-_build","content":"`input_wav`","locale":"en"},"9271":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-260","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#buffers-_build","content":"`extra + crossfade + sola_search + block`, rolling buffer at the device sample rate","locale":"en"},"9272":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-261","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#buffers-_build","content":"`input_wav_res`","locale":"en"},"9273":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-262","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#buffers-_build","content":"16 kHz version of the same buffer","locale":"en"},"9274":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-263","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#buffers-_build","content":"`skip_head` / `return_length`","locale":"en"},"9275":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-264","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#buffers-_build","content":"`extra_frame // zc` / `(block + sola_buffer + sola_search) // zc`","locale":"en"},"9276":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-265","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#per-block-processing-_process-and-_infer","content":"HuBERT runs on the whole `context` (longer context stabilises features), but retrieval only handles new frames after `skip_head // 2`, and synthesis generates only new audio (`skip_head`/`return_length`), keeping latency manageable.","locale":"en"},"9277":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-266","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#per-block-processing-_process-and-_infer","content":"F0 is computed only on the buffer tail: length `block_frame_16k + 800`, rounded up to a multiple of 5120 then minus 160 for RMVPE (its frame alignment). After removing a few frames at both ends, results roll into the 1024-frame `cache_pitch`/`cache_pitchf`. Live pm differs from offline: both ends are zero-padded using a 65 Hz lower bound, then trimmed to the block's frame count (`_pm`), matching the original's live implementation.","locale":"en"},"9278":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-267","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#per-block-processing-_process-and-_infer","content":"Formant: `return_length2 = ceil(return_length · factor)`, `cache_pitchf` is multiplied by `return_length2 / return_length`, then generated audio is resampled back using `upp_res`—the offline method comes from here.","locale":"en"},"9279":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-268","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#per-block-processing-_process-and-_infer","content":"SOLA (`audio/sola.py`, from DDSP-SVC): finds the offset within `sola_search_frame` with the highest normalised cross-correlation to the previous block's tail, fades in/out with `sin²`, and saves the tail for the next block.","locale":"en"},"9280":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-269","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#per-block-processing-_process-and-_infer","content":"Resampling from device sample rate to 16 kHz and from synthesizer sample rate to device sample rate both use the ported `sinc_resample.Resample`, through CUDA Graph (`realtime-input-resample`, `realtime-output-resample`).","locale":"en"},"9281":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-270","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#two-differences-from-the-original","content":"**Speaker selection works**: the original's `rtrvc.py` hardcodes `sid` to 0, preventing speaker selection for multi-speaker voices in the live GUI and VST; here it uses `params.speaker_id`, clamped to `[0, n_spk−1]`.","locale":"en"},"9282":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-271","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#two-differences-from-the-original","content":"**Changing block size does not reload the model**: see the next section.","locale":"en"},"9283":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-272","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hot-updates","content":"Call","locale":"en"},"9284":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-273","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hot-updates","content":"Effect","locale":"en"},"9285":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-274","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hot-updates","content":"`update(VoiceParams)`","locale":"en"},"9286":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-275","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hot-updates","content":"Pitch, formant, index strength, protect, loudness mix, speaker and F0 method apply from the next block","locale":"en"},"9287":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-276","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hot-updates","content":"`reconfigure(StreamParams)`","locale":"en"},"9288":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-277","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hot-updates","content":"Threshold and denoising switches update live; changes to `block_ms`, `crossfade_ms`, `context_ms` (`StreamParams.same_buffers()` is false) rebuild buffers while keeping the voice loaded, with about one silent block","locale":"en"},"9289":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-278","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hot-updates","content":"`set_voice(voice, index)`","locale":"en"},"9290":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-279","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hot-updates","content":"Switch voice between blocks; HuBERT and F0 models are shared by the runtime","locale":"en"},"9291":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-280","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hot-updates","content":"`set_index(index)` / `reset()`","locale":"en"},"9292":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-281","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hot-updates","content":"Change index / clear all buffers (after switching devices)","locale":"en"},"9293":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-282","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hot-updates","content":"`prewarm()`","locale":"en"},"9294":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-283","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hot-updates","content":"Run a block of 220 Hz test tone through every stage to trigger CUDA Graph capture and lazy loading, then `reset()`","locale":"en"},"9295":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-284","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hot-updates","content":"`passthrough` property","locale":"en"},"9296":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-285","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hot-updates","content":"Skip the voice and return (gated, denoised) input, replacing the original's `im` mode","locale":"en"},"9297":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-286","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hot-updates","content":"All entry points share an `RLock`, so control-thread updates cannot interleave with the processing thread's `process()`.","locale":"en"},"9298":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-287","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#hot-updates","content":"`prepare_voice()` calls `remove_weight_norm()` on the voice's `net_g`, matching the original's live loader (folding weight normalisation into weights for faster inference). This modifies the runtime's cached object in place, so `StreamEngine` is used only in the Live worker's own `Runtime`, never the server process's runtime.","locale":"en"},"9299":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-288","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#latency-estimation","content":"`stream/latency.py:estimate_latency_ms()` extends the original's display formula (output latency + block + crossfade + 10 ms, plus the input denoising term `min(crossfade, 40 ms)`): it also adds one block prefilled in the output ring buffer, one block of target ring-buffer fill for split streams, and input device latency. Measured inference time is reported separately in statistics (`infer_ms_p50/p95`).","locale":"en"},"9300":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-289","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#audio-codecs-and-dsp","content":"Module","locale":"en"},"9301":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-290","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#audio-codecs-and-dsp","content":"Contents","locale":"en"},"9302":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-291","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#audio-codecs-and-dsp","content":"`audio/io.py`","locale":"en"},"9303":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-292","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#audio-codecs-and-dsp","content":"`probe()` (duration, sample rate, channels, codec; counts frames for containers without duration), `decode()` (PyAV + libswresample decode, mix and resample in one pass, with `max_seconds` for previews), `encode()` (soundfile for wav/flac, PyAV transcoding for mp3/m4a; writes `.<name>.part` then renames), `to_int16()`. Falls back to `ffmpeg`/`ffprobe` commands (if on PATH) when PyAV fails. The original's `clean_path` quote/space stripping was removed—paths are already clean here, and any path accepted by the operating system works","locale":"en"},"9304":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-293","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#audio-codecs-and-dsp","content":"`audio/resample.py`","locale":"en"},"9305":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-294","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#audio-codecs-and-dsp","content":"`resample()`: librosa's default (soxr) resampling, for the post-conversion “resample to” option, matching the original","locale":"en"},"9306":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-295","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#audio-codecs-and-dsp","content":"`audio/sinc_resample.py`","locale":"en"},"9307":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-296","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#audio-codecs-and-dsp","content":"`Resample`, `sinc_resample_kernel`, `apply_sinc_resample_kernel` ported from torchaudio 2.11, bit-identical (see Parity with the original)","locale":"en"},"9308":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-297","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#audio-codecs-and-dsp","content":"`audio/dsp.py`","locale":"en"},"9309":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-298","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#audio-codecs-and-dsp","content":"`highpass_16k()` (48 Hz 5th-order Butterworth, zero-phase), `change_rms()` (original loudness envelope blending), dB conversion","locale":"en"},"9310":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-299","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#audio-codecs-and-dsp","content":"`audio/peaks.py`","locale":"en"},"9311":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-300","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#audio-codecs-and-dsp","content":"`compute_peaks()`: interleaved (min, max) pairs for waveform display, cached by core in `cache/peaks/`","locale":"en"},"9312":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-301","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#audio-codecs-and-dsp","content":"`audio/sola.py`","locale":"en"},"9313":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-302","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#audio-codecs-and-dsp","content":"SOLA crossfade","locale":"en"},"9314":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-303","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#audio-codecs-and-dsp","content":"`audio/denoise.py`","locale":"en"},"9315":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-304","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#audio-codecs-and-dsp","content":"TorchGate spectral gate denoising, copied from the original's `tools/torchgate/` (reuses `STFT` from `f0/rmvpe.py`)","locale":"en"},"9316":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-305","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#audio-codecs-and-dsp","content":"`audio/effects.py`","locale":"en"},"9317":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-306","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#audio-codecs-and-dsp","content":"The effects chain over pedalboard (an optional dependency, imported only here): `EFFECTS`, the catalog of kinds with each parameter's default and range (pedalboard's own names; the core validates against it and `GET /convert/effects` serves it); `apply()` for a file, which pads a reverb's or delay's tail and cuts it at −80 dB; `EffectsChain` for a stream, which keeps the plugins' state between blocks and always returns a whole block. Pitch shift is offline only (`streams=False`): streamed, pedalboard's holds back about a second. `unavailable_reason()` first imports pedalboard in a child process (once): some of its wheels stop the importing process with an illegal instruction on older processors, and effects are then reported unavailable instead","locale":"en"},"9318":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-307","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#audio-codecs-and-dsp","content":"PyAV uses the same libavformat/libswresample libraries that the original invokes through `ffmpeg`, so no system ffmpeg installation is required. mp3 and AAC accept only specific sample rates; `_transcode()` uses `encoder_rate()` to raise 40 kHz output to 44.1 kHz—the original's `wav2` omits this, so mp3/m4a export from 40k models fails.","locale":"en"},"9319":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-308","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#real-time-audio-devices-audio_io","content":"Device handling in the original's `realtime_gui.py` has several problems: the same microphone appears once per Windows driver (MME, DirectSound, WASAPI, WDM-KS), requiring users to select a driver type first; MME truncates names to 31 characters, while the GUI saves and matches by name; a single duplex `sd.Stream` requires the same driver type for input and output; missing saved devices silently switch to system defaults; refreshing the list stops conversion and calls private `sd._terminate()`/`sd._initialize()`; changing any device stops streams and reloads the model. `audio_io/` rewrites this handling to address these problems.","locale":"en"},"9320":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-309","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#enumeration-and-grouping-devicespy","content":"`enumerate_raw()` is the only function importing `sounddevice`: it calls `query_hostapis()`, `query_devices()`, then probes support for 44.1/48/88.2/96 kHz with `check_input_settings`/`check_output_settings`. It runs in the short-lived `workers.devices` subprocess, so re-enumeration does not disturb active streams.","locale":"en"},"9321":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-310","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#enumeration-and-grouping-devicespy","content":"`group_devices()` and `resolve_selection()` are pure functions, tested with captured real enumeration results (`tests/fixtures/devices/{windows,macos,linux_pipewire}.json`):","locale":"en"},"9322":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-311","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#enumeration-and-grouping-devicespy","content":"Name normalisation (`normalize_name`): case folding, collapsing whitespace, stripping `(<host api>)` suffixes added by some drivers.","locale":"en"},"9323":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-312","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#enumeration-and-grouping-devicespy","content":"Group names from longest to shortest, so an MME name truncated to 31 characters can match the full name by prefix; ambiguous prefixes form separate groups—the cost is duplicate display, safer than combining different devices.","locale":"en"},"9324":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-313","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#enumeration-and-grouping-devicespy","content":"Each group is a physical device, with `variants` sorted by driver rank and the recommended driver first. Ranks (`HOST_API_RANKS`, lower is better): Windows ASIO → WASAPI → WDM-KS → DirectSound → MME; macOS Core Audio; Linux JACK → ALSA → Pulse → OSS.","locale":"en"},"9325":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-314","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#enumeration-and-grouping-devicespy","content":"Device id is `sha1(host_api | original name | direction | same-name ordinal)[:12]`, stable across enumeration; `portaudio_index` is valid only for the current enumeration and is never saved.","locale":"en"},"9326":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-315","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#enumeration-and-grouping-devicespy","content":"Aliases following system defaults, such as `Microsoft Sound Mapper` and `Primary Sound Driver`, are omitted; virtual devices (VB-Audio, VoiceMeeter, BlackHole, Loopback, Soundflower, null sink…) get `is_virtual`.","locale":"en"},"9327":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-316","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#enumeration-and-grouping-devicespy","content":"`resolve_selection(selection, device_list, direction)` recovers saved selections in a new list, trying in order: same `device_id` → same physical key and driver → recommended driver for the same physical key → same name on any driver → system default. It returns `exact`, `matched`, `default_fallback` or `missing`, with an explanation for the last two. Core decides whether output fallback is allowed (`allow_output_fallback`), because sending converted audio to speakers instead of a virtual cable is an immediately noticeable error.","locale":"en"},"9328":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-317","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#sessions-and-topology-streamspy","content":"`AudioSession` opens input, output and optional monitor output on a `Backend`, connects them with ring buffers, and runs a processing thread passing whole blocks to a processor (usually `StreamEngine.process`).","locale":"en"},"9329":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-318","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#sessions-and-topology-streamspy","content":"**Engine sample rate** (`engine_rate`): selected output sample rate; otherwise the output device's default if supported; otherwise 48 kHz. This replaces the original's confusing `sr_model`/`sr_device` switch.","locale":"en"},"9330":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-319","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#sessions-and-topology-streamspy","content":"**Topology** (`choose_topology`): one duplex stream (`duplex`, lowest latency) when input and output share a driver and both support the rate; otherwise separate input + output streams (`split`), with drift correction in the ring buffer between them. If input does not support the engine rate, it opens at its own default rate and converts with `LinearResampler` linear interpolation—the engine resamples to 16 kHz anyway, so this interpolation has little cost. Monitor output is always a separate output stream branched from the processing thread (`monitor_source`: `converted`, `input` or `both`).","locale":"en"},"9331":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-320","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#sessions-and-topology-streamspy","content":"**Channels**: input opens through the highest selected channel (mono by default), with selected channels mixed to mono in the callback; output writes mono to selected channels (first two by default). Some drivers open nothing narrower than the device itself (a WDM-KS pin, a stereo-only card) and refuse mono with `Invalid number of channels [PaErrorCode -9998]`. Enumeration therefore probes the channel counts too (`probe_device`: 1, 2 and the maximum at the default rate, every count up to 8 when one of those fails) and keeps them as `channel_counts` when the device refuses some. A stream then opens the next accepted count up (`accepted_channels`), leaving the extra channels out, and supported rates are probed at that count.","locale":"en"},"9332":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-321","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#sessions-and-topology-streamspy","content":"**Callbacks only move data and update counters**: no torch, logging or large allocations. The output ring buffer is prefilled with one silent block at startup (two for `split`).","locale":"en"},"9333":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-322","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#sessions-and-topology-streamspy","content":"**Device loss**: reported when a stream's `finished_callback` fires unexpectedly or the watchdog sees no callback for a role for longer than `max(0.5 s, 3 blocks)` (`on_device_lost(role, device_id, reason, message)`), only once. `classify_portaudio_error()` maps PortAudio error text to `missing`, `busy`, `channels` (-9998, kept apart: another rate cannot fix it), `format`, `permission`.","locale":"en"},"9334":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-323","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#sessions-and-topology-streamspy","content":"**Statistics**: input/output peak and RMS, inference time p50/p95 (last 50 blocks), underrun/overrun counts, drift ppm for `split`.","locale":"en"},"9335":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-324","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#sessions-and-topology-streamspy","content":"`Ring` (`rings.py`) is a single-producer, single-consumer float32 ring buffer; the lock covers only index operations and two slice copies, so the audio callback waits for the processing thread for no longer than one copy. Writing to a full buffer counts as an overrun; reading an empty one as an underrun.","locale":"en"},"9336":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-325","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#sessions-and-topology-streamspy","content":"`DriftCorrector` (`drift.py`) handles the fact that two device clocks never stay perfectly synchronised: the reader exponentially smooths ring-buffer fill; when it deviates from target by more than half a block, it removes or duplicates one sample every 10 ms (at the quietest point in each 10 ms segment) until within 1/8 block of target. Target fill is measured after the first second, because the relative phase of the two callbacks determines the stable fill level, which no constant can predict. Real clock drift is tens of ppm, a few samples per second, and is inaudible.","locale":"en"},"9337":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-326","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#sessions-and-topology-streamspy","content":"`SounddeviceBackend` uses PortAudio (`latency=\"low\"`, WASAPI through `WasapiSettings`: exclusive when chosen, otherwise shared with `auto_convert`, so Windows converts channel count and rate where PortAudio alone converts only mono ↔ stereo); `enable_asio` sets `SD_ENABLE_ASIO=1` to select the ASIO-enabled PortAudio bundled on Windows since sounddevice 0.5.1. `fake.py:FakeBackend` simulates devices: one thread per stream calls callbacks at the device's own clock (`1 + ppm·1e-6` times the nominal rate), `lose(index)` simulates unplugging a USB device, `refuse` makes opening fail, and output is recorded for assertions. Tests and worker requests with `backend: \"fake\"` use it (`backend_from_request`). `tone.py:chime()` is the chime played by the “Test” button.","locale":"en"},"9338":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-327","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#recording-an-output-device-capturepy","content":"PortAudio cannot record a render endpoint: an output device has no input channels, so opening one as an input fails with `Invalid number of channels [PaErrorCode -9998]`. PortAudio's WASAPI host API gained loopback devices (each output listed again as an input named `<output> [Loopback]`, opened with `AUDCLNT_STREAMFLAGS_LOOPBACK`) in 2022, after the 19.7.0 release whose DLL sounddevice bundles; its ALSA host API cannot name a PulseAudio monitor. So a loopback provider records instead: `SoundcardLoopback` over SoundCard (≥ 0.4.3, which returns silence instead of blocking while nothing plays), WASAPI loopback on Windows and monitor sources on PulseAudio/PipeWire. macOS has no provider (Core Audio's process taps are outside PortAudio); `loopback_provider()` says so, and the message reaches `DeviceList.errors`.","locale":"en"},"9339":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-328","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#recording-an-output-device-capturepy","content":"**Listing**: with `live.show_all_devices` the enumeration asks for loopback sources (`\"loopback\": true`). `add_loopback_sources` appends them as input devices of a `Loopback` host API with negative indexes, `loopback_source` (the provider's id) and `loopback_of` (the output's name). PortAudio devices that already are loopbacks (`[Loopback]`, `Monitor of …`, `devices.loopback_target`) are used as they are, and the provider is not asked. A loopback input's physical key starts with `loopback:`, so it never groups with a microphone of the same name; without the setting the core hides loopback inputs.","locale":"en"},"9340":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-329","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#recording-an-output-device-capturepy","content":"**Opening**: `SounddeviceBackend.open_input` and `check` send an endpoint with `loopback_source` to the provider. `CaptureStream` opens, reads (`record(None)`, whatever arrived) and closes the recorder on its own thread (in the COM multithreaded apartment on Windows), waits for the open so a refusal is a `DeviceOpenError`, calls the input callback from `start()`, and reports the device lost if the recorder dies. The provider converts any rate and channel count; `choose_topology` always splits.","locale":"en"},"9341":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-330","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#recording-an-output-device-capturepy","content":"**Feedback**: the core refuses (`feedback`, at check, Start, a device change and passthrough) a loopback input whose `loopback_of` names the output or monitor; by name, exact on Windows.","locale":"en"},"9342":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-331","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"`workers/live.py` is a persistent process owning audio devices and real-time threads, isolated from the server's event loop, GIL contention and crashes. It connects back to core with `multiprocessing.connection.Client` (address and random authkey in the request file, `protocol/live.py:WorkerRequest`), exchanging JSON messages defined in `protocol/live.py`.","locale":"en"},"9343":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-332","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"To the worker","locale":"en"},"9344":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-333","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"Purpose","locale":"en"},"9345":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-334","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"`Start(config)`","locale":"en"},"9346":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-335","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"Load voice (`voice_path`, `index_path`), build engine, prewarm, open devices; states `starting` → `loading` → `prewarming` → `running`","locale":"en"},"9347":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-336","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"`Stop` / `Shutdown`","locale":"en"},"9348":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-337","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"Stop session / exit process","locale":"en"},"9349":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-338","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"`UpdateVoice(params)`","locale":"en"},"9350":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-339","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"Hot update; changing F0 method first loads the new model in the control thread; missing assets keep the old method and report `asset_missing`","locale":"en"},"9351":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-340","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"`UpdateStream(stream)`","locale":"en"},"9352":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-341","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"Hot-update threshold and denoising; block size and similar changes close streams, `reconfigure`, reopen streams, without reloading the voice","locale":"en"},"9353":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-342","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"`SetVoice(voice_path, index_path, speaker_id)`","locale":"en"},"9354":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-343","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"Switch voice while running: the old voice keeps converting while the new one loads; loading failure reports `detail.reason == \"voice_load\"` and retains the old voice. Success or failure returns `State` with `voice_path`, telling core which voice is actually converting","locale":"en"},"9355":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-344","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"`SetDevices(devices)`","locale":"en"},"9356":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-345","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"Only close and reopen streams; rebuild the engine only if engine sample rate changes (the voice remains in the runtime cache)","locale":"en"},"9357":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-346","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"`Meter(on, input, input_gain_db)`","locale":"en"},"9358":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-347","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"Open the input alone while idle to measure its level; asked again for the same input, keep the stream and take the new gain. The core decides when (lease, state, retries)","locale":"en"},"9359":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-348","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"`TestTone(device)`","locale":"en"},"9360":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-349","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"Play a chime on an output","locale":"en"},"9361":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-350","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"`Passthrough(on)`","locale":"en"},"9362":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-351","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"Pass input straight to monitor (or output) to check routing","locale":"en"},"9363":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-352","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"`ReleaseMemory`","locale":"en"},"9364":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-353","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"Unload engine and all models; ignored during an active session","locale":"en"},"9365":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-354","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"From the worker","locale":"en"},"9366":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-355","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"Contents","locale":"en"},"9367":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-356","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"`State`","locale":"en"},"9368":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-357","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"`state`, `error` (`{code, message, detail}`), `topology`, `sample_rate`, `meter`, `passthrough`, `cached` (models loaded in the worker runtime, for “Free GPU memory”), `voice_path`","locale":"en"},"9369":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-358","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"`Stats`","locale":"en"},"9370":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-359","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"10 Hz levels (input, output, monitor), inference time, block size, estimated latency, underruns/overruns, drift, GPU memory","locale":"en"},"9371":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-360","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"`DeviceLost`","locale":"en"},"9372":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-361","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"Role, device id, reason, message","locale":"en"},"9373":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-362","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"`Log`","locale":"en"},"9374":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-363","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"Logs","locale":"en"},"9375":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-364","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#live-worker-and-control-protocol","content":"After device loss, the worker reports `DeviceLost`, stops the session and enters `reconnecting`, then waits for `SetDevices`; core handles re-enumeration and reopening on `exact`/`matched` (see Core services). On a processing-thread error or OOM during loading/prewarming, the worker unloads all its models first, then reports `compute_unavailable` (`detail.reason = \"out_of_memory\"`).","locale":"en"},"9376":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-365","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#vocal-separation","content":"**Model table and presets** (`separate/presets.py`): `MODELS` is the original's `MODEL_SPECS` in `tools/pymss_webui.py`—five Roformer models (`vocals`, `vocals-aggressive`, `dereverb`, `dereverb-aggressive`, `karaoke`), each with checkpoint, YAML, primary/secondary output stems and labels, batch size, overlap, size and SHA-256. Presets are data in `core/separation/presets.json`: five single-model presets plus two chains, `vocals-clean` (vocals → dereverb) and `lead-clean` (vocals → karaoke → dereverb). YAML is packaged (`separate/configs/`), so only checkpoints need downloading; `resolve_model()` prefers YAML found beside `pymss_weights/` in the asset directory, allowing direct use of an original installation.","locale":"en"},"9377":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-366","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#vocal-separation","content":"**Chain planning** (`plan_chain`): each step separates the previous step's primary stem; the final primary stem is written with the preset's `primary_stem` label, and other stems are written if their labels appear in preset `stems`, each label only once. Output names are `<input>.<label>.<format>`, with `-2`, `-3` on conflicts.","locale":"en"},"9378":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-367","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#vocal-separation","content":"**Runner** (`separate/runner.py:run_separation`): decodes each input once to 44.1 kHz stereo, separates step by step according to the plan and writes stems; models load only once per job; progress is “audio seconds processed / total seconds × steps”. `PymssSeparator` uses pymss 2.1.7's `MSSeparator` with the same options as the original; pymss recognises only cpu/cuda/mps, so on DirectML the model loads on CPU before moving to the device (converted to half precision for the fp16 attempt).","locale":"en"},"9379":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-368","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#vocal-separation","content":"**DirectML fp16 → fp32 retry**: DirectML tries fp16 first; a retryable error (other than OOM) before any file succeeds raises `RetryInFp32`, causing the worker to exit with `75` (`EXIT_RETRY`). Core reruns in a new process with `precision: \"fp32\"`—matching the original design, because a failed DirectML context cannot recover in-process.","locale":"en"},"9380":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-369","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#vocal-separation","content":"**Import check**: the `check` action in `workers/separate.py` loads an imported model once on CPU, rejecting incorrect checkpoint/YAML pairs during import. `inspect_config()` identifies YAML using pymss_core's `detect_model_type`, and `custom_model()` produces the same description shape as `resolve_model()` for imported models.","locale":"en"},"9381":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-370","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#vocal-separation","content":"`FakeSeparator` splits input in half into two stems, with configurable delay and fp16 failure for tests.","locale":"en"},"9382":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-371","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"Core adds an optional `clean` stage before these stages (cleaning the dataset with a separation preset); it calls the separation service rather than engine training code. See Core services.","locale":"en"},"9383":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-372","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"Each stage is a function: it accepts a frozen request dataclass, writes to the experiment folder, reports progress and logs through callbacks, and skips completed work. Stages run in `workers.train` subprocesses (one process per stage; cancellation kills the process tree); parallel portions use `train/parallel.py:run_parts()` to start subprocesses with `spawn` (fork breaks CUDA and some BLAS implementations), running in-process when there is only one part.","locale":"en"},"9384":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-373","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"Stage","locale":"en"},"9385":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-374","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"Function","locale":"en"},"9386":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-375","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"Output (inside experiment folder)","locale":"en"},"9387":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-376","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"Notes","locale":"en"},"9388":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-377","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"`slice`","locale":"en"},"9389":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-378","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"`train/preprocess.py:run(SliceRequest)`","locale":"en"},"9390":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-379","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"`0_gt_wavs/`, `1_16k_wavs/`, `slice_done.txt`","locale":"en"},"9391":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-380","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"slicer2 (−42 dB), about 3.7 s per slice with 0.3 s overlap, causal high-pass then normalisation before writing; **every slice's tail is written** (fixing an original regression); completed input keys go in `slice_done.txt`, so only unfinished inputs are re-sliced after interruption","locale":"en"},"9392":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-381","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"`f0`","locale":"en"},"9393":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-382","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"`train/f0_extract.py:run(F0Request)`","locale":"en"},"9394":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-383","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"`2a_f0/` (coarse), `2b-f0nsf/` (continuous)","locale":"en"},"9395":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-384","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"Only `pm` (CPU multiprocessing) and `rmvpe` (one device, or one process per GPU with `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES`); no pitch shift; entirely unvoiced clips are skipped","locale":"en"},"9396":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-385","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"`features`","locale":"en"},"9397":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-386","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"`train/feature_extract.py:run(FeatureRequest)`","locale":"en"},"9398":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-387","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"`3_feature256/` or `3_feature768/`","locale":"en"},"9399":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-388","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"Shares `extract_features` with inference","locale":"en"},"9400":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-389","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"`fit`","locale":"en"},"9401":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-390","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"`train/loop.py:run(TrainRequest)`","locale":"en"},"9402":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-391","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"`G_*.pth`, `D_*.pth`, `weights/*.pth`, `metrics.jsonl`, `filelist.txt`, `config.json`","locale":"en"},"9403":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-392","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"Single GPU or CPU in-process; multiple GPUs use DDP over gloo, one spawn process per card","locale":"en"},"9404":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-393","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"`index`","locale":"en"},"9405":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-394","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"`index/build.py:run(IndexRequest)`","locale":"en"},"9406":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-395","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"`trained_*.index`, `added_*.index`, `total_fea*.npy`, `index_fingerprint.json`","locale":"en"},"9407":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-396","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"See “Retrieval indexes” above","locale":"en"},"9408":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-397","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"Details of `fit`:","locale":"en"},"9409":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-398","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"`train/filelist.py` writes `filelist.txt` like the original's `run_train_model` (`gt.wav|feature.npy|f0.npy|f0nsf.npy|speaker_id`, omitting two columns without pitch guidance, appending `|speaker_name` for multiple speakers), repeating rows according to each speaker's repeat count and adding two silent sample rows per speaker. Silent samples are packaged (`engine/train/data/mute/`, same contents as the original's `mute.zip`) and copied to `<experiment>/mute/`, because the data loader caches `.spec.pt` alongside them.","locale":"en"},"9410":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-399","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"Hyperparameters are in `CONFIGS` in `train/hparams.py` (the original's `configs/v1|v2/*.json`); v2 has no 40k file, so it uses `v1/40k`.","locale":"en"},"9411":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-400","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"Resume from the latest G/D and continue with **subsequent** epochs (the original repeats the saved epoch); otherwise load base models, expanding `emb_g` when speaker count exceeds the base model's (`load_pretrained_generator`). AMP is enabled only when every selected card qualifies for fp16 (`training_is_half`, the original's `get_training_dtype`).","locale":"en"},"9412":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-401","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"The original's `os._exit(0)`, `argparse` and global state were removed; progress, logs, metrics and saved files are reported through `Sink` (direct callbacks in-process, rank 0 through a queue for DDP). Each metrics line is written to `metrics.jsonl` and sent as a `metric` event, converted by core to `train_metrics`. TensorBoard is optional. Small models go in `<experiment>/weights/`, no longer in global `assets/weights/`.","locale":"en"},"9413":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-402","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#training","content":"Other helpers: `train/multispeaker.py` (speaker table, `Name_ID_Repeat` folder convention, `multispeaker_manifest.json`, slice files named by `output_key` to carry speakers through F0, features, file lists and indexes); `train/scan.py:scan_dataset()` (reports clip count, duration, sample rates and issues such as too short/too long/clipped before slicing); `train/experiment.py` (stage output existence, checkpoint lists, reading `metrics.jsonl`, reading original experiment folders with `read_legacy()`, default batch size of smallest GPU memory in GiB ÷ 2, default CPU process count of cores ÷ 1.5).","locale":"en"},"9414":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-403","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#fingerprints","content":"`train/fingerprint.py:of_paths(paths, extra)` hashes relative names, sizes and modification times of the given files and directories (recursively) with SHA-1, mixing in `extra` (settings determining output, such as sample rate and F0 method) as text; missing paths hash differently from empty folders. It reads metadata rather than contents, so even multi-GB datasets are instant. It has two users: the index stage uses it to decide whether to rebuild (see “Retrieval indexes” above); core records a fingerprint for each completed stage in `experiment.json`. “Run all” compares each stored fingerprint with a freshly computed one and reruns a stage whose fingerprint changed, together with every stage after it; settings edits and upstream reruns also mark stages stale explicitly (see Core services).","locale":"en"},"9415":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-404","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#worker-subprocess-entry-points","content":"All four workers use `python -m rvc_next.workers.<name> <request.json>` and share `workers/base.py:run_worker()`:","locale":"en"},"9416":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-405","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#worker-subprocess-entry-points","content":"Duplicate real stdout for events, then redirect fd 1 to stderr—third-party `print` output stays as log text instead of entering the event stream.","locale":"en"},"9417":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-406","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#worker-subprocess-entry-points","content":"Events are JSON lines prefixed with `@rvc ` (`protocol/jsonl.py`); `parse_line()` treats unprefixed lines as info logs. Types are in `protocol/messages.py`: `progress`, `step`, `log`, `metric`, `output`, `result`, `error`.","locale":"en"},"9418":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-407","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#worker-subprocess-entry-points","content":"`rvc_next` logger output becomes `log` events; `SIGTERM` exits with 130 (`EXIT_CANCELLED`).","locale":"en"},"9419":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-408","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#worker-subprocess-entry-points","content":"Exception mapping: `Cancelled` → 130; `MissingAssetError` → `asset_missing` (with `detail.assets`); `ModelFormatError` → `invalid_model`; OOM → `compute_unavailable` (`detail.reason = \"out_of_memory\"`); other errors → `internal_error`, first sending the full traceback as a `log` event.","locale":"en"},"9420":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-409","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#worker-subprocess-entry-points","content":"Worker","locale":"en"},"9421":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-410","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#worker-subprocess-entry-points","content":"Request","locale":"en"},"9422":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-411","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#worker-subprocess-entry-points","content":"Result","locale":"en"},"9423":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-412","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#worker-subprocess-entry-points","content":"`train`","locale":"en"},"9424":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-413","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#worker-subprocess-entry-points","content":"`{\"stage\": …}` plus the stage request fields, `assets_dir`, `device`, `precision`","locale":"en"},"9425":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-414","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#worker-subprocess-entry-points","content":"Stage result dictionary","locale":"en"},"9426":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-415","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#worker-subprocess-entry-points","content":"`separate`","locale":"en"},"9427":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-416","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#worker-subprocess-entry-points","content":"Inputs, preset, resolved models, output directory and format, device and precision; or `{\"action\": \"check\", \"model\": …}`","locale":"en"},"9428":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-417","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#worker-subprocess-entry-points","content":"`{outputs, failed, processed, device, precision}`; exit code 75 means rerun in fp32","locale":"en"},"9429":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-418","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#worker-subprocess-entry-points","content":"`devices`","locale":"en"},"9430":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-419","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#worker-subprocess-entry-points","content":"`{\"action\": …}`, action `enumerate`, `check` or `test_tone`","locale":"en"},"9431":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-420","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#worker-subprocess-entry-points","content":"Device list, or `{ok, reason, message, role, …}`","locale":"en"},"9432":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-421","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#worker-subprocess-entry-points","content":"`live`","locale":"en"},"9433":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-422","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#worker-subprocess-entry-points","content":"`WorkerRequest` (address, authkey, asset directory, device, precision, `cuda_graph`, `enable_asio`, backend)","locale":"en"},"9434":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-423","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#worker-subprocess-entry-points","content":"None; all communication through the connection","locale":"en"},"9435":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-424","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#worker-subprocess-entry-points","content":"See Architecture and Core services for process startup and how events become job progress.","locale":"en"},"9436":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","type":"page","content":"Parity with the original","breadcrumbs":["RVC Next Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-parity","locale":"en"},"9437":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity","content":"RVC Next rewrites the orchestration, storage, process control, and all interfaces of Retrieval-based-Voice-Conversion-WebUI (the original, ~/codeworkspace/Retrieval-based-Voice-Conversion-WebUI, commit 81eed5e), but ports the numerical code rather than reinventing it*","locale":"en"},"9438":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-rules","content":"Porting rules","locale":"en"},"9439":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-parity#golden-tests","content":"Golden tests","locale":"en"},"9440":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-parity#offline-scriptsgoldenpy","content":"Offline: `scripts/golden.py`","locale":"en"},"9441":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-parity#pytest-testsgolden","content":"pytest: `tests/golden/`","locale":"en"},"9442":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-parity#proven-results","content":"Proven results","locale":"en"},"9443":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Intentional behavior changes","locale":"en"},"9444":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-torchaudio-and-torchfcpe","content":"Porting torchaudio and torchfcpe","locale":"en"},"9445":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-parity#original-codebase-overview","content":"Original codebase overview","locale":"en"},"9446":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-parity#entry-points","content":"Entry points","locale":"en"},"9447":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-parity#key-implementation-locations","content":"Key implementation locations","locale":"en"},"9448":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-parity#formats-that-must-remain-readable","content":"Formats that must remain readable","locale":"en"},"9449":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-parity#issues-found-in-the-original-and-their-resolution","content":"Issues found in the original and their resolution","locale":"en"},"9450":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity","content":"RVC Next rewrites the orchestration, storage, process control, and all interfaces of Retrieval-based-Voice-Conversion-WebUI (the “original” below, `~/code_workspace/Retrieval-based-Voice-Conversion-WebUI`, commit `81eed5e`), but **the numerical code is ported, not reinvented**: users' existing voices must sound the same as in the original. This page explains the porting rules, how parity is proven (golden tests), intentional behavior changes, and the original's structure for maintainers comparing the two.","locale":"en"},"9451":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity","content":"The original is a heavily modified 2026 branch, not upstream: it replaces fairseq HuBERT with transformers, UVR5 with PyMSS, and adds CUDA Graph acceleration, multi-speaker training, a CLI, and a VST plugin. “Original” below always means this branch.","locale":"en"},"9452":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-rules","content":"**Copied directly**: these files are copied from the original with only import paths fixed, preserving their shape for line-by-line diffs:","locale":"en"},"9453":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-rules","content":"rvc-next","locale":"en"},"9454":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-rules","content":"Original","locale":"en"},"9455":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-rules","content":"`engine/models/{synthesizer,modules,attentions,commons,transforms}.py`","locale":"en"},"9456":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-rules","content":"`infer/module/*.py` (four synthesizer classes and two discriminator types)","locale":"en"},"9457":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-rules","content":"`engine/f0/rmvpe.py`","locale":"en"},"9458":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-rules","content":"`infer/rmvpe.py`","locale":"en"},"9459":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-rules","content":"`engine/audio/denoise.py`","locale":"en"},"9460":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-rules","content":"`tools/torchgate/` (TorchGate)","locale":"en"},"9461":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-rules","content":"`engine/graph.py`","locale":"en"},"9462":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-rules","content":"`tools/cuda_graph.py` (only the switch changes from an environment variable to a process-level setting)","locale":"en"},"9463":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-rules","content":"`engine/train/{slicer,losses,mel}.py`","locale":"en"},"9464":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-rules","content":"`train/dataset/slicer2.py`, `train/losses.py`, `train/mel_processing.py`","locale":"en"},"9465":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-rules","content":"`engine/train/dataset.py`","locale":"en"},"9466":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-rules","content":"`train/data_utils.py`","locale":"en"},"9467":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-rules","content":"**Ported line by line to new interfaces**: pipeline mathematics stays unchanged, wrapped in new functions and classes—offline conversion (`engine/convert/offline.py` ← `infer/vc/pipeline.py`), streaming conversion (`engine/stream/engine.py` ← `infer/rtrvc.py` plus `start_vc`/`audio_callback` in `realtime_gui.py`), training stages (`engine/train/*` ← `train/*.py`, `train/dataset/*.py`, `tools/multispeaker.py`, file-list code in `webui.py`), checkpoint tools (`engine/models/{checkpoint,merge,extract}.py` ← `train/process_ckpt.py`), GPU rules (`engine/runtime.py` ← `configs/config.py`), separation model table (`engine/separate/` ← `tools/pymss_webui.py`).","locale":"en"},"9468":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-rules","content":"**Not ported**: Gradio handlers, live GUI callbacks and controls, process launching (`Popen(cmd, shell=True)` with `%`-formatted command lines), Chinese status strings, engine interfaces returning Gradio update dictionaries.","locale":"en"},"9469":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-rules","content":"To keep copied files comparable with the original, `pyproject.toml` excludes `engine/models/{attentions,commons,modules,transforms,synthesizer}.py` and `engine/f0/rmvpe.py` from `ruff format` and ty, and applies per-file ignores for `B`, `SIM`, `UP`, `E402`, `E712`, `E741`, `F841`, and other rules to these files plus `engine/audio/denoise.py` and `engine/train/{dataset,losses,mel,slicer}.py` (see Development environment). Preserve the original style when editing these files; use golden tests to confirm bug fixes do not change numerical results.","locale":"en"},"9470":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#golden-tests","content":"Golden tests run the original and rvc-next on the same machine and compare outputs. Both use **CPU, fp32**, the same random seed, and the same 16 kHz input (decoded only once).","locale":"en"},"9471":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#offline-scriptsgoldenpy","content":"`scripts/golden.py` adds the original checkout to `sys.path`, importing only `infer.hubert`, `infer.vc.pipeline`, and `infer.module.models` (no interfaces), and builds `Pipeline` with the original CPU chunking parameters (`x_pad/x_query/x_center/x_max = 1/6/38/41`); rvc-next uses `Runtime(device=\"cpu\", precision=\"fp32\")` and `OfflineConverter`. Each (voice, clip, F0 method) produces one line: RMS sample difference (on a −1..1 scale) and whether the output is bit-identical. Exceeding `TOLERANCE = 1e-4` gives exit code 1. Without `--f0`, only `pm` and `rmvpe` are compared.","locale":"en"},"9472":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#pytest-testsgolden","content":"Both tests carry the `golden` marker, deselected by default (`addopts = \"-m 'not live and not golden and not assets'\"`); run with `pytest -m golden`. Environment variables:","locale":"en"},"9473":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#pytest-testsgolden","content":"Variable","locale":"en"},"9474":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#pytest-testsgolden","content":"Meaning","locale":"en"},"9475":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#pytest-testsgolden","content":"`RVC_NEXT_GOLDEN_ORIGINAL`","locale":"en"},"9476":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#pytest-testsgolden","content":"Original checkout, default `~/code_workspace/Retrieval-based-Voice-Conversion-WebUI`","locale":"en"},"9477":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#pytest-testsgolden","content":"`RVC_NEXT_TEST_ASSETS`","locale":"en"},"9478":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#pytest-testsgolden","content":"Asset directory in the original layout (`hubert_base/`, `rmvpe/`; offline tests also need `fcpe/fcpe_c_v001.pt`)","locale":"en"},"9479":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#pytest-testsgolden","content":"`RVC_NEXT_GOLDEN_VOICES`","locale":"en"},"9480":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#pytest-testsgolden","content":"`.pth` list separated by `os.pathsep`","locale":"en"},"9481":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#pytest-testsgolden","content":"`RVC_NEXT_GOLDEN_CLIP`","locale":"en"},"9482":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#pytest-testsgolden","content":"An audio file","locale":"en"},"9483":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-46","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#pytest-testsgolden","content":"`test_offline_golden.py`: runs `pm`, `rmvpe`, and `fcpe` for each voice through `scripts/golden.py`, with pitch shift 2 and speaker 1; all must be within 1e-4 **and bit-identical**. The original's FCPE depends on torchfcpe, so the test uses `importorskip(\"torchfcpe\")`—torchfcpe must be installed in the test environment for the original side; rvc-next itself does not depend on it.","locale":"en"},"9484":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-47","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#pytest-testsgolden","content":"`test_stream_golden.py`: the original `start_vc`/`audio_callback` live in the GUI's `__main__` block and cannot be imported, so the test has a GUI-free copy (`OriginalStream`) driving the original `rtrvc.RVC`; rvc-next uses `StreamEngine`. Compare each voice block by block (up to 12 blocks), with the same seed before each block. Three parameter sets cover 48k/40k, RMVPE/pm, pitch shifts, formant 1.0, input/output denoising, and thresholds. The original live code imports torchaudio, so the test uses `importorskip(\"torchaudio\")`.","locale":"en"},"9485":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-48","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#pytest-testsgolden","content":"Tests skip rather than fail when the original checkout, assets, or required packages are missing (CI lacks these original dependencies).","locale":"en"},"9486":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-49","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#proven-results","content":"As recorded in AGENTS.md, on CPU fp32:","locale":"en"},"9487":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-50","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#proven-results","content":"**Offline**, pm and RMVPE: four voice types—v1 40k, v2 40k, v2 40k without pitch, v2 48k—on speech, silence, and a 70-second clip exceeding the chunking threshold, with pitch shift and speaker ID, produce **bit-identical** output; FCPE is also bit-identical.","locale":"en"},"9488":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-51","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#proven-results","content":"**Streaming**: `StreamEngine` is bit-identical block by block to `rtrvc.RVC.infer` plus the live GUI callback mathematics (gate, input/output TorchGate, formant, RMS mix, SOLA).","locale":"en"},"9489":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-52","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#proven-results","content":"HuBERT, RMVPE, and FCPE weight initialization (later overwritten by checkpoints) consumes the global RNG, while the original builds RMVPE/FCPE inside `Pipeline`. If one side seeds before building models and the other does the reverse, subsequent random sequences (such as the NSF noise source) diverge, and results cease to be bit-identical. Golden code therefore calls `runtime.hubert()` and `runtime.f0(method)` first; the original side also builds RMVPE/FCPE in advance and assigns them to `pipeline.model_rmvpe`/`model_fcpe`, then calls `torch.manual_seed()`. Keep this order in new golden cases.","locale":"en"},"9490":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-53","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Each change has a reason found in the original code; with default parameters, changes affecting numerical results do not alter golden results.","locale":"en"},"9491":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-54","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Change","locale":"en"},"9492":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-55","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Original","locale":"en"},"9493":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-56","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"rvc-next","locale":"en"},"9494":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-57","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Reason","locale":"en"},"9495":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-58","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Slicing keeps every slice's tail","locale":"en"},"9496":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-59","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"The final `norm_write(tmp_audio…)` in `train/preprocess.py` sits outside `for audio in self.slicer.slice(audio)`, writing only the last segment's tail (regression introduced in `81eed5e`; inside the loop in `5d47da1`)","locale":"en"},"9497":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-60","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"`PreProcess.slices()` collects tails inside the loop","locale":"en"},"9498":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-61","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Every slice's tail was silently dropped from the original training dataset","locale":"en"},"9499":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-62","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Live honors the speaker","locale":"en"},"9500":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-63","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"`sid` hard-coded to 0 in `infer/rtrvc.py`","locale":"en"},"9501":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-64","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Uses `VoiceParams.speaker_id`","locale":"en"},"9502":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-65","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Multi-speaker voices allow speaker selection in Live","locale":"en"},"9503":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-66","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Rebuild indexes when data changes","locale":"en"},"9504":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-67","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"`train/train_index.py` skips if any `added_*.index` exists","locale":"en"},"9505":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-68","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Checks feature fingerprints (`index_fingerprint.json`)","locale":"en"},"9506":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-69","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"The original kept using old indexes after retraining on changed data","locale":"en"},"9507":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-70","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Load indexes once","locale":"en"},"9508":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-71","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"`infer/vc/pipeline.py` calls `faiss.read_index` + `reconstruct_n` for every file","locale":"en"},"9509":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-72","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"`Runtime.index()` caches by path and modification time","locale":"en"},"9510":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-73","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Slow batch conversion","locale":"en"},"9511":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-74","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"pm, rmvpe, fcpe everywhere","locale":"en"},"9512":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-75","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Original CLI accepts only pm and rmvpe, inference UI also has fcpe","locale":"en"},"9513":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-76","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"UI, CLI, API, and Live offer all three, plus CREPE and SwiftF0 (from Applio's choice of methods); training still only pm and rmvpe","locale":"en"},"9514":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-77","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Consistent parameter behavior everywhere","locale":"en"},"9515":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-78","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"HuBERT normalization","locale":"en"},"9516":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-79","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Training feature extraction applies `layer_norm` when `do_normalize` is true; inference never does","locale":"en"},"9517":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-80","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"One shared function, `features/hubert.normalize_input`, for training and inference","locale":"en"},"9518":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-81","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Real assets have `do_normalize: false`, so neither normalizes and results remain unchanged","locale":"en"},"9519":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-82","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Offline formant shift","locale":"en"},"9520":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-83","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Only available live","locale":"en"},"9521":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-84","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Offline uses the live method (pitch at `key − formant`, generate `ceil(n·2^(f/12))` frames with scaled NSF pitch, then resample back)","locale":"en"},"9522":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-85","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"The parameter works alike in Convert and Live; bit-identical when `formant = 0`","locale":"en"},"9523":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-86","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Any path","locale":"en"},"9524":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-87","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Live GUI rejects paths with non-ASCII characters; `clean_path` strips quotes","locale":"en"},"9525":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-88","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Any path accepted by the OS","locale":"en"},"9526":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-89","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"—","locale":"en"},"9527":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-90","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Inference outside the audio callback","locale":"en"},"9528":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-91","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Live GUI performs the entire conversion in the `sounddevice` callback","locale":"en"},"9529":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-92","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Callback only moves data; a processing thread runs the engine","locale":"en"},"9530":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-93","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Avoid audio glitches; callbacks must not block","locale":"en"},"9531":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-94","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Change block length without stopping the stream to reload","locale":"en"},"9532":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-95","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Block, crossfade, or context changes require stopping the stream","locale":"en"},"9533":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-96","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Rebuild buffers, keep the voice loaded","locale":"en"},"9534":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-97","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"—","locale":"en"},"9535":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-98","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Resume training","locale":"en"},"9536":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-99","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Repeats the saved epoch after restoration","locale":"en"},"9537":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-100","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Continues from the next epoch","locale":"en"},"9538":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-101","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"—","locale":"en"},"9539":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-102","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"mp3/m4a output","locale":"en"},"9540":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-103","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"`wav2` leaves sample rate unchanged; 40k models fail to export mp3/m4a","locale":"en"},"9541":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-104","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Raise to the nearest encoder-supported rate (40 kHz → 44.1 kHz)","locale":"en"},"9542":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-105","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"—","locale":"en"},"9543":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-106","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"Other differences visible during comparison, without being behavior changes:","locale":"en"},"9544":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-107","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"**Offline output is up to two 10 ms frames shorter**—the original's per-chunk flooring; preserved for bit-identical results.","locale":"en"},"9545":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-108","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"**transformers 5**: the original pins below 4.50; rvc-next needs `transformers>=5,<6` (4.x requires `huggingface_hub < 1`). HuBERT layer outputs are unchanged and golden runs are bit-identical.","locale":"en"},"9546":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-109","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"**Index k-means** uses faiss rather than scikit-learn MiniBatchKMeans (only above 200,000 vectors), removing a dependency; same purpose, different numbers.","locale":"en"},"9547":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-110","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#intentional-behavior-changes","content":"**CUDA Graph switches**: the original uses `RVC_CUDA_GRAPH`; rvc-next uses settings `compute.cuda_graph_offline` (default off) and `compute.cuda_graph_live` (default on); the cache capacity variable is renamed to `RVC_NEXT_CUDA_GRAPH_MAX_CACHE`. Golden code clears `RVC_CUDA_GRAPH` before running.","locale":"en"},"9548":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-111","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-torchaudio-and-torchfcpe","content":"The original uses torchaudio for live resampling and torchfcpe for FCPE. torchaudio's last version is 2.11, and it declares no torch dependency, so unconstrained torch can resolve to a version it cannot load; torchfcpe also pulls in torchaudio. rvc-next therefore ports the parts it uses and no longer depends on either package:","locale":"en"},"9549":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-112","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-torchaudio-and-torchfcpe","content":"Port","locale":"en"},"9550":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-113","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-torchaudio-and-torchfcpe","content":"Source","locale":"en"},"9551":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-114","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-torchaudio-and-torchfcpe","content":"Used for","locale":"en"},"9552":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-115","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-torchaudio-and-torchfcpe","content":"Evidence","locale":"en"},"9553":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-116","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-torchaudio-and-torchfcpe","content":"`engine/audio/sinc_resample.py`: `Resample`, `sinc_resample_kernel`, `apply_sinc_resample_kernel`","locale":"en"},"9554":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-117","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-torchaudio-and-torchfcpe","content":"torchaudio 2.11 `transforms.Resample` and `functional._get_sinc_resample_kernel`/`_apply_sinc_resample_kernel` (BSD-2, full license in the file header)","locale":"en"},"9555":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-118","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-torchaudio-and-torchfcpe","content":"Streaming input→16 kHz, voice→device sample rate, two formant resampling steps, non-16 kHz FCPE input","locale":"en"},"9556":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-119","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-torchaudio-and-torchfcpe","content":"`tests/engine/test_sinc_resample.py`: kernels and outputs satisfy `torch.equal` against torchaudio at rate pairs rvc-next actually uses (48000→16000, 44100→16000, 40000→48000, 471→400, 504→480, etc.); Kaiser windows also match","locale":"en"},"9557":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-120","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-torchaudio-and-torchfcpe","content":"`engine/f0/fcpe_model.py`: `Wav2Mel`, convolution-only `CFNaiveMelPE`, decoders, `load_fcpe`","locale":"en"},"9558":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-121","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-torchaudio-and-torchfcpe","content":"torchfcpe (FCPE, MIT; conformer from Diffusion-SVC)","locale":"en"},"9559":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-122","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-torchaudio-and-torchfcpe","content":"`fcpe` F0 method","locale":"en"},"9560":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-123","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-parity#porting-torchaudio-and-torchfcpe","content":"`tests/engine/test_fcpe_model.py`: mel and F0 are bit-identical to torchfcpe with both `argmax` and `local_argmax` decoding (unvoiced frames are NaN on both sides); bundled weights match those shipped with torchfcpe","locale":"en"},"9561":{"id":"/en/docs/development-parity-124","page_id":"/en/docs/development-parity","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-p