t to speech"],[6507,"Text to speech: `tts`"],[6939,"Text written into the model"],[6962,"Text written into the model"],[7143,"TF32 maths on NVIDIA GPUs; saved"],[10762,"That program takes the device in exclusive mode: turn off exclusive control for it"],[10378,"The “accompaniment” added back is preferably the `instrumental` stem; if the preset has no accompaniment stem, the other stem is used. For example, with **Remove reverb**, the separated reverb is added back, equivalent to “convert the dry vocals, then add the original reverb back”."],[11170,"The **Base models** tab says: **Training starts from a base model. Download the official ones below, or import your own G (and D); their rate, version and pitch guidance are read from the weights.**"],[11769,"The **Data folder** path;"],[12100,"The **effects** chain on Convert and Live (reverb, compressor, delay and more) needs pedalboard, an optional extra:"],[11982,"The **Export as voice** button at the top right of the list exports the latest result (preferring the latest small model, otherwise the latest G checkpoint)."],[10841,"The **Hear yourself** switch sends the original input straight to the monitor device (or to the output if no monitor is set), so you can check the “microphone → headphones” routing."],[10345,"The **Inputs** section offers three ways to add audio, which you can combine:"],[12105,"The **Live** page uses PortAudio through `sounddevice` to access sound cards."],[11977,"The **Loss** chart shows training loss curves (the horizontal axis is step). Total losses `g_total` and `d_total` come first, followed by components such as `mel`, `kl`, `fm` and `gen`. Curves update live during training. **Gradient norms** below shows `grad_g` and `grad_d`, the size of the generator's and the discriminator's gradients at each step: a sudden jump often comes before a run goes wrong."],[11243,"The **Presets** menu at the top right."],[10868,"The **round trip** is measured: the bursts go through the same buffers a session uses (the output prefill, the device's buffers both ways, the cable or the air). The **conversion** part is the converter's own delay (crossfade + 10 ms, plus min(crossfade, 40 ms) with input noise reduction). It depends only on the settings, so it is computed, and SOLA may take up to 10 ms off it. Inference time adds nothing as long as there are no underruns."],[11768,"The **rvc-next** version;"],[11176,"The **Separation models** tab says: **The built-in models are downloaded here or in Assets; import your own as a checkpoint with its YAML config. Each becomes a preset in Separate.**"],[10958,"The **Start / Stop** button. From the moment you click it, it shows each step of starting: **Checking the audio devices…**, **Starting the live process…** (first start only), **Loading PyTorch and the compute device…** (first start only), **Loading the voice…**, **Loading the index…**, **Loading HuBERT…**, **Loading the pitch model…**, **Warming up…**; steps with nothing to do are skipped. While stopping it shows **Stopping…**. With no voice selected, it is disabled and says **Choose a voice to start.**"],[11771,"The **torch** version and its CUDA version."],[10369,"The **Voice parameters** panel contains **Pitch method**, **Pitch**, **Formant**, **Index strength**, **Consonant protection** and **Loudness match**. The **Presets** menu at the top right applies or saves presets. On Convert, the panel says “Changes apply to the next conversion.” Click **Preview 15 s** or **Convert** after editing to use the new values."],[11049,"The **Voices** tab lists all voices in the library as cards. Each card shows the name, sample rate · version · **pitch guidance**/**no pitch guidance**, and labels:"],[11078,"The **Voices**, **Base models** and **Separation models** tabs each have a drop zone at the top. All three use the same import workflow, so **any drop zone works**: rvc-next identifies each file by its **contents** (rather than its name or extension), then decides where it belongs."],[9777,"The `dev` dependency group does not pin `ruff` or `ty`; the latest releases are used. Each `select` rule in `[tool.ruff.lint]` is listed explicitly: ruff upgrades change the default rule set, so listing the rules keeps an upgrade from changing which rules run (a newer release may still judge a rule more strictly). `ANN` requires a type hint on every function in `rvc_next/` (`Any` allowed); the ported files, tests and scripts are exempt. `tomli` is also in `dev`, allowing ty to resolve the 3.10 fallback import on newer Python versions."],[8238,"The `live` group (devices, stream parameters, last voice and parameters) lives in settings rather than client state because devices belong to the server machine and the CLI's `live run` needs the same values. Pure UI preferences (theme, language, last page) are client state. See Configuration and environment variables for each group's fields."],[8129,"The `open_services()` context manager in `cli/output.py` constructs services once per command and closes them afterwards"],[8445,"The `rvc-next` CLI is a thin shell over the core: each command parses arguments, calls one core method, and prints a table or JSON. This page explains its organization and the rules for adding commands. See Command reference for each command's options, and Configuration and environment variables for settings and environment variables."],[7738,"The `rvc-next` command line handles the same operations as the Web UI: conversion, vocal separation, training, live voice conversion, model and asset management, presets, job queries, and settings. Both call the same core services (one core method per operation), so the command line offers the same capabilities as the interface, and its `--json` output is identical to the JSON returned by the API."],[8443,"The `webui` command"],[7164,"The 48 Hz high-pass before slicing; saved"],[10520,"The access token is optional; other computers can reach the server without one. If the server has an access token set, an **Access token** dialog appears when the browser first connects; enter it to proceed. Only operations such as **Show in folder** require the browser and server to be on the same computer. See Remote access and security."],[8227,"The API layer also defines `NotLocalError` (`not_local`, 403) for operations such as “Show in folder” that only make sense for a browser on the server machine."],[9728,"The API side runs `python -m rvc_next webui --no-open --port <api-port> --strict-port`. `--strict-port` is used because Vite's proxy target is fixed at startup: if the API silently switches to the next port, Vite keeps proxying to the wrong address. Use another `--api-port` if the port is occupied (for example, another RVC Next is already on 7868)."],[8073,"The API's JSON and CLI's `--json` output therefore have exactly the same shape: `test_model_json_matches_api_shape` in `tests/cli/test_cli.py` directly compares `rvc-next model list --json` with `GET /api/v1/models`. `Record` sets `json_schema_serialization_defaults_required`, marking defaulted fields as required in the output schema, so the generated TypeScript types match what the server actually sends."],[10728,"The app's own noise cancellation, where it has one"],[8255,"The asset catalog: `catalog.json`"],[11464,"The audio stream briefly rebuffers after a change"],[11468,"The audio stream briefly rebuffers after a change"],[11472,"The audio stream briefly rebuffers after a change"],[10760,"The block's `type` must match a plugin you have: `type pipewire` needs the **pipewire** device, `type pulse` the **pulse** device. `pactl list short sinks` must list `rvc_next`"],[10390,"The bottom action bar has two buttons:"],[10861,"The bottom of the panel has a status line, for example `48 kHz · one duplex stream · est. latency 640 ms`:"],[10057,"The browser pauses rendering during the update callback, including `requestAnimationFrame`, so the callback must finish on Vue's `nextTick` (when the new view is in the DOM), never wait for a frame—an early frame-based wait froze navigation."],[6480,"The browser sends the proxy's address as its `Origin`, which differs from the forwarded `Host`; add it to `server.allowed_origins`;"],[11617,"The browser then opens the **Access token** dialog (**This server asks for its access token.**). Enter the same token to continue."],[10236,"The browser will open this address automatically shortly afterward. The default port is **7868** (separate from the original RVC's 7865). To stop the server, press ctrl+c in the terminal."],[10744,"The browser's microphone must then be your real microphone, and every other sound the browser plays goes into the virtual device too."],[10989,"The button then reads **Stop recording · 0:12**. The recording ends when you stop it, when Live stops, or when the sample rate changes; it then appears under **Recordings** on the Live page and in every outputs list, with the voice and parameters it was made with. Recording happens off the audio thread and never makes a block late. Output and monitor levels do not apply to it."],[6388,"The callback only moves data; inference runs on a separate thread"],[10299,"The catalog currently contains `kikiV1`, `keruanV1`, `guanguanV1` (v1, 40 kHz), `zhanzhanv2-xi` (v2, 40 kHz) and `youzhanv2-xi` (v2, 48 kHz; no index was officially released for it)."],[6601,"The certificate's private key"],[7426,"The certificate's private key, when the certificate file does not include it"],[8681,"The CLI calls `run_now()`: direct execution on the current thread, with Ctrl+C triggering cancellation and killing the subprocess tree, recording the job as `cancelled`."],[7927,"The client address is loopback and the `Host` is a loopback name (a public host name forwarded by a tunnel or a same-machine reverse proxy is not; `testclient` in tests also counts)"],[6478,"The code has no specific support for running behind a reverse proxy and no configurable path prefix. If you do use one, account for the checks above:"],[10206,"The code has three sources, each handled differently:"],[6288,"The command line has no separate release command: each command's process exits when finished, releasing its GPU memory."],[6470,"The command line is not restricted by the browsing scope: someone running commands on the server already has access to these files."],[6204,"The command line is not subject to this restriction."],[10419,"The command line starts from the voice's default preset. Use `--preset` to choose another preset, then override individual values with `--pitch`, `--index-rate`, etc. `--overwrite` can overwrite matching filenames only when you specify an output folder with `-o`. See Command reference for all options."],[10322,"The Convert page changes audio files to the selected voice. It handles single files, multiple files or an entire folder, and can separate vocals before conversion and add the accompaniment back afterward to make a song cover in one operation."],[10811,"The converted audio goes here. **Output level** below it shows what rvc-next sends to this device (the converted voice, or the input with **Hear yourself**); it reads **—** while nothing plays. The **Test** button beside it plays a tone on this device. To send the audio to another program, choose a virtual cable's playback side (Use Live in other apps); to convert what an output device plays, see Record an output device."],[10986,"The converted voice alone, mono"],[11411,"The corrector comes from Applio."],[12090,"The CUDA 13.0 build needs a current NVIDIA driver (version 580 or newer)."],[11770,"The current **Device**;"],[8463,"The current command tree:"],[10458,"The current step and the completed step count for multi-step jobs, such as `2/5`;"],[6466,"The data directory;"],[12113,"The data folder location;"],[6104,"The database, models, experiments, results, uploads, caches, and `server.json`"],[11379,"The default 0.33 suits most cases;"],[11619,"The default address `127.0.0.1` allows only local access. After you change **Server address** to `0.0.0.0` or a LAN IP, no access token is needed: anyone who can connect can use every feature (browsing the server folders allowed below included). Do this only on a trusted network, add protection of your own such as a firewall or a VPN, or set an access token. See Remote access and security."],[6149,"The default port is 7868. If it is in use, the server automatically tries 7869, 7870, … (up to 20 ports), logs \"Port 7868 is unavailable. Using port 7869.\", and opens the browser on the actual port."],[9861,"The default suite runs offline conversion and streaming with `pm` and `fcpe`; RMVPE has no tiny version and only runs in tests with real assets"],[11929,"The default, **Chosen from the rate, version and pitch guidance**, uses the corresponding official base model."],[9668,"The development environment is `.venv` at the repository root (listed in `.gitignore`), created with uv from a Python 3.11 interpreter, without inheriting system site-packages:"],[10876,"The device cannot be found"],[11643,"The device panel here is identical to the one on **Live**; both edit the same settings. See “Audio devices” in Live for instructions."],[10804,"The device panel is titled **Audio devices on \\{host}**, reminding you that these devices belong to the server machine. If you open the page remotely from another computer, it also shows: **These devices belong to the server machine, not this browser.** The **Refresh devices** button at the top right rescans devices. While stopped, the panel also refreshes automatically every 5 seconds when the page is visible."],[10888,"The device rejects the current sample rate"],[11128,"The dialog begins with: **Some files could not be paired with certainty. Check the choices below; only compatible voices are offered for each index.** Files are grouped by type:"],[9736,"The documentation site (home page and these docs) is a separate frontend project under `site/`, following Hanafubuki's website structure: Fumadocs on TanStack Start and Vite, dependencies managed with bun, building to static files."],[10891,"The driver accepts the settings but ends the stream once it runs (a disabled or disconnected device, one held exclusively by another program, or a driver fault)"],[10885,"The driver will not open the device with this many channels (rvc-next already picks a count the device reported accepting, so this usually means exclusive mode or a device that changed)"],[10389,"The effects are not stored in presets; the command line takes them as `--effect` (`convert`)."],[9317,"The effects chain over pedalboard (an optional dependency, imported only here): `EFFECTS`, the catalog of kinds with each parameter's default and range (pedalboard's own names; the core validates against it and `GET /convert/effects` serves it); `apply()` for a file, which pads a reverb's or delay's tail and cuts it at −80 dB; `EffectsChain` for a stream, which keeps the plugins' state between blocks and always returns a whole block. Pitch shift is offline only (`streams=False`): streamed, pedalboard's holds back about a second. `unavailable_reason()` first imports pedalboard in a child process (once): some of its wheels stop the importing process with an illegal instruction on older processors, and effects are then reported unavailable instead"],[7382,"The engine also reads `RVC_NEXT_CUDA_GRAPH_MAX_CACHE`: the maximum number of CUDA Graphs cached per call site (default: 8). Usually there is no need to change it."],[10864,"The estimate relies on the latency the drivers report, which is often optimistic. **Measure latency** (below the status line) measures it instead:"],[11791,"The experiment name, with a **Running** label while active;"],[11988,"The experiment's indexes (`added_*.index`) are attached to the new voice;"],[11990,"The exported voice appears in **Models › Voices** and can be used in **Convert**, **Live** and other pages."],[8776,"The extension only selects a reader; content determines what the file is:"],[6160,"The FCPE model (`fcpe`, about 41 MB) is an asset in the default `inference` download group, so `rvc-next assets download` with no arguments fetches it too. If only FCPE is missing, the UI prompts you to download it when you select FCPE; on the command line you can also run `rvc-next assets download fcpe` on its own."],[9021,"The FCPE port"],[11867,"The file cannot be decoded"],[6164,"The file's SHA-256 differs from the catalog, usually because the download is incomplete or the file was replaced. Download the asset again in **Models** › **Assets**, or run `rvc-next assets download <id>`. `rvc-next assets verify` rechecks all installed assets."],[9496,"The final `norm_write(tmp_audio…)` in `train/preprocess.py` sits outside `for audio in self.slicer.slice(audio)`, writing only the last segment's tail (regression introduced in `81eed5e`; inside the loop in `5d47da1`)"],[10694,"The first command creates the sink **RVC-Next** (`rvc_next`), the second the source **RVC-Next-Microphone** (`rvc_next_mic`), which carries whatever is played into the sink. Check them with `pactl list short sinks` and `pactl list short sources`."],[10822,"The first item is always **System default**, following the operating system's current default device."],[10677,"The first time rvc-next opens the microphone, macOS asks whether the program that started rvc-next (Terminal, iTerm2, VS Code…) may use it. Allow it. If you refused, or rvc-next reports **Microphone permission denied**, turn that program on in **System Settings › Privacy & Security › Microphone**, then quit it completely and start rvc-next again."],[6360,"The folder must contain `0_gt_wavs` or `config.json`. The `eval` folder is skipped when copying. Stages whose outputs already exist (slice, pitch, features, fit, index) are marked complete; you can then run unfinished stages or increase the epoch count to continue training. If an experiment folder has no `experiment.json`, RVC Next reads it as an original experiment."],[11927,"The fp16 loss scale is saved with the checkpoints, so a resumed run continues from it instead of starting the scale again. Trained voices carry the epoch, step and date, and the author from **Settings › Training › Author** when set; **About** on a voice's page shows them."],[7902,"The frontend uses `XMLHttpRequest` to `send(file)` directly and receive upload progress (`fetch()` cannot provide it)."],[8029,"The full picture of RVC Next: package structure and layer rules, service assembly, process responsibilities, a request's round trip from browser to engine, and shared infrastructure for errors, settings, events, the database and data directory."],[7830,"The generated client groups endpoints by tag; each route declares its error shape"],[11361,"The goal is to keep the shifted pitch within **the range of the voice's training data**. Straying too far can make the voice sound weak, off-key or distorted."],[6167,"The GPU does not meet the GPU rules (less than 4 GiB of memory or insufficient compute capability); see Hardware and performance;"],[6138,"The host enters the child app's `lifespan` on its own event loop (it starts forwarding events to the socket) and calls `services.close()` when it exits. `start_background=True` starts the job scheduler and idle unloading; without it, queued jobs do not run."],[6097,"The host program must run in the same Python environment as RVC Next, and that environment must meet RVC Next's requirements (see Installation). Do not install it into a shared environment with conflicting dependencies."],[10984,"The input on the left channel, the converted voice on the right: for comparing them, or for editing either one later"],[10882,"The input records the output (or monitor) that plays the converted voice"],[11363,"The interface adjusts in steps of 1 semitone; the command line and API accept decimals, such as `--pitch 1.5`. **Pitch** affects only voices with pitch guidance."],[11449,"The interface does not yet support deleting or renaming presets; use the command line:"],[10439,"The interface has three parts:"],[11421,"The interval rounded to a semitone (at most two octaves). Good for speech."],[11424,"The interval rounded to whole octaves, so a song stays in its key. Good for singing."],[6290,"The job fails with **The GPU is not available or ran out of memory** (`compute_unavailable`, with `detail.reason` set to `out_of_memory`; command-line exit code 9);"],[6632,"The job's folder in the data directory"],[6733,"The job's folder in the data directory"],[8677,"The last GPU job releases the lease when it ends (`finally` in `_thread_main`)."],[7250,"The last voice used"],[10801,"The latency is the one shown plus the network's round trip and the 60 ms buffer. A wired or good Wi-Fi network on the same site keeps it small; the line under the title shows the buffer and the dropouts."],[7192,"The latest one"],[7822,"The lifecycle (`lifespan`) only starts and stops the socket bridge. The caller creates and closes the services: `rvc-next webui` uses `build_services(start_background=True)` to start job scheduling and idle unloading, then passes them to `create_app`; tests pass their own `Services`. The same `create_app` can thus serve a real process or run inside `TestClient`."],[10387,"The limiter also raises the level as it limits (pedalboard's limiter has make-up gain); put a **Gain** before it to aim it."],[11010,"The live background process starts on first use and stays running. If ASIO devices fail to open after toggling the ASIO setting, restart the rvc-next server so the live process picks up the new setting."],[10783,"The Live chip in the top bar"],[6384,"The live GUI rejects non-ASCII paths"],[10767,"The Live page converts microphone audio into the selected voice in real time, then sends it to headphones, speakers or a virtual audio device (for streaming or voice chat, for example). From top to bottom, the page has three sections: audio devices, voice and parameters, and buffering and noise reduction. The Start / Stop button and status bar stay fixed at the bottom."],[10264,"The main contents are:"],[10975,"The measured latency, when a measurement fits the session's devices and block length"],[10869,"The measurement stays valid while you keep the same input, output and block length. While a session runs with them, the status bar shows **measured N ms** beside the estimate, and the Live chip uses it. Changing the crossfade or noise reduction only recomputes the conversion part; changing a device or the block clears the measurement."],[10727,"The meeting's audio settings, or the site's microphone permission"],[10515,"The memory-module icon in the top bar shows GPU memory usage. Hover over it to see “GPU memory: used of total · Loaded: …” (or the device in use when there is no GPU). Its badge shows the number of models currently loaded in memory."],[6140,"The methods match the API one to one and return models shaped like the API's JSON; see Core services."],[10809,"The microphone. The **Input level** meter below it shows its volume in real time whenever the device panel is shown, before the first **Start**, during a session and after it stops (an error included). It opens in the background, so the page never waits for it, and it comes back by itself once an unplugged or busy microphone is available again. You can turn it off in **Settings › Audio › Show the input meter while stopped**."],[10901,"The middle section contains the **Voice** picker and **Voice parameters** panel, using the same controls as **Convert**. See Voice parameters for their meanings. On the Live page:"],[11880,"The model that reads what is said. The others come from Applio: SPIN keeps less of the speaker's own voice in the features, the language HuBERTs suit those languages. All but ContentVec and SPIN are for v2 only. The voice records its choice, so conversion uses the same; the model downloads on first use (about 380 MB)"],[11014,"The Models page manages all model files used by rvc-next: your voices, base models for training, vocal separation models, and required assets such as HuBERT and RMVPE. There are five tabs at the top:"],[11334,"The neural pitch tracker many RVC voices were trained with; a smooth contour on singing, but about real time on a CPU"],[10797,"The next time you open the page, your previous devices, voice, parameters and buffer settings are restored automatically. Just click **Start**."],[8410,"The official repository contains two kinds of files: weights themselves and 21 Windows distributions (`RVC*.7z`, from 2023-03 to 2026-07, each a complete installation). A full inventory was taken of revision `e6d0c1a` (155 files, about 103 GB): index every `.7z`, compare model files over 50 KB (`.pth .pt .ckpt .onnx .bin .safetensors .index`, excluding bundled `runtime/` and legacy `TEMP/`) with repository files by size, then content; for files found only inside archives, extract by CRC-32, compute SHA-256, and actually load them. Findings:"],[8327,"The official RVC repository; no demo voices, FCPE or CREPE weights"],[9152,"The offline converter's `_empty_cache()` calls `torch.cuda.empty_cache()` only when CUDA Graph is disabled: captured graphs' memory pools must be retained, or replay fails. This matches the original's `Pipeline`."],[9150,"The only difference from the original is the switch: the original reads `RVC_CUDA_GRAPH`; here `Runtime` calls `configure_cuda_graph(device, wanted)` at construction to set a process-wide switch, enabled only after the device passes a capture–replay probe (`detect_cuda_graph_support`). `wanted` comes from settings: the server uses `compute.cuda_graph_offline` (off by default, matching the original's offline default), and the Live worker uses `compute.cuda_graph_live` (on by default). Training never enables it."],[11850,"The original always filters; turn it off only for recordings already filtered"],[11844,"The original brings every piece to the same peak; per file keeps the differences inside a recording; as recorded keeps the levels"],[9092,"The original executes GPU rules and parses `argparse` arguments when `configs/config.py` is imported (the CLI and VST worker must therefore clear `sys.argv` first). Here it is a pure function, `choose_device(requested, precision) -> DeviceChoice`:"],[11835,"The original finds the silences first and cuts the sound between them into pieces. Straight through keeps silences in the pieces. As they are suits a dataset you cut yourself; a file longer than the piece length + 5 s is still cut, since training would leave it out"],[9451,"The original is a heavily modified 2026 branch, not upstream: it replaces fairseq HuBERT with transformers, UVR5 with PyMSS, and adds CUDA Graph acceleration, multi-speaker training, a CLI, and a VST plugin. “Original” below always means this branch."],[9506,"The original kept using old indexes after retraining on changed data"],[11368,"The original RVC offers formant shifting only in live voice conversion. RVC Next applies the same method to file conversion as well; at **Formant** 0, the computation is identical to the original."],[9195,"The original skips building whenever any `added_*.index` exists, leaving an old index after training on new data. Here the fingerprint of each feature file group (`train/fingerprint.py:of_paths`, including version and name) is stored in `index_fingerprint.json`; old files are removed and rebuilt only if the fingerprint changes or `force=True`, otherwise it returns `reused: true`."],[9250,"The original supports formant shift only in the live engine; here its live method is ported to offline conversion:"],[9190,"The original uses FCPE through `torchfcpe.spawn_bundled_infer_model`. `f0/fcpe_model.py` ports only the path this model actually executes: `Wav2Mel` (128-bin log-mel, 16 kHz, hop 160), convolution-only `CFNaiveMelPE` (`ConvOnlyEncoder` stacking `ConformerConvModule`), two decoders and `load_fcpe()`. The attention branch (depending on `einops`, `local_attention`) never runs for `fcpe_c_v001.pt` with `conv_only: true`, so it was not ported; non-16 kHz input is resampled by the ported `sinc_resample.Resample`, matching torchfcpe's use of torchaudio. Arithmetic is unchanged; output is bit-identical to torchfcpe (`tests/engine/test_fcpe_model.py`). This removes rvc-next's dependency on torchfcpe (which pulls in torchaudio), with weights downloaded as the `fcpe` asset (see Assets and model repositories for sources)."],[9548,"The original uses torchaudio for live resampling and torchfcpe for FCPE. torchaudio's last version is 2.11, and it declares no torch dependency, so unconstrained torch can resolve to a version it cannot load; torchfcpe also pulls in torchaudio. rvc-next therefore ports the parts it uses and no longer depends on either package:"],[9412,"The original's `os._exit(0)`, `argparse` and global state were removed; progress, logs, metrics and saved files are reported through `Sink` (direct callbacks in-process, rank 0 through a queue for DDP). Each metrics line is written to `metrics.jsonl` and sent as a `metric` event, converted by core to `train_metrics`. TensorBoard is optional. Small models go in `<experiment>/weights/`, no longer in global `assets/weights/`."],[6190,"The other program must record the virtual device's *recording* side (**CABLE Output**, **Voicemeeter Out B1**, **BlackHole 2ch**, **RVC-Next-Microphone**), not **Default** and not your real microphone."],[10748,"The other program must use the *recording* side (**CABLE Output**, **Voicemeeter Out B1**, **BlackHole 2ch**, **RVC-Next-Microphone**), rvc-next's Output the *playback* side. Live must be **Running**. Voicemeeter: the AUX strip's **B1** is on, and Voicemeeter is running"],[10750,"The other program records the real microphone (or **Default**); in Voicemeeter, the microphone's strip sends to **B1**"],[10565,"The other program's microphone"],[10747,"The other side hears nothing"],[6188,"The output device used last time is disconnected (for example, headphones or a sound card were unplugged). The UI reports **The saved output device is missing; the system default would be used.** Click **Start with the default output** to confirm, or choose another device in the device panel. On the command line, add `--allow-fallback`. With **Reconnect a lost device automatically** enabled, a device lost during a session resumes automatically when reconnected."],[10422,"The overall layout of the RVC Next web interface and the parts shared across pages: navigation, top bar, bottom action bar, job cards, results list and handoffs between pages. Learning these makes the guides for individual pages much easier to follow."],[10340,"The page has three sections from top to bottom, with a fixed action bar at the bottom:"],[11500,"The page remembers your last selected preset. If you have never selected one, it uses **Default separation preset** under **Settings › Training defaults** (initially **Vocals**)."],[11239,"The panel is titled **Voice parameters** and contains:"],[10902,"The panel says **Changes apply as you drag.** While running, adjusting **Pitch**, **Formant**, **Index strength**, **Consonant protection** or **Loudness match**, or switching **Pitch method**, immediately affects the converted audio without interrupting it."],[9522,"The parameter works alike in Convert and Live; bit-identical when `formant = 0`"],[11389,"The pitch is filled in from the neighbouring frames, and **Consonant protection** applies to these frames."],[8814,"The plan originally proposed committing as a job with a new `imports_changed` event; in practice everything is local copying, with separation trial loading the slowest step (seconds), so it directly returns `ImportResult` (`voices`, `attached`, `inbox`, `base_models`, `separation_models`, `checkpoints`, `skipped`)."],[9754,"The principle in `pyproject.toml`: the **lower bound** is the version the code needs or a dependency requires; the **upper bound** stops before the next untested major version. torch is the exception and has no upper bound, since users install the build for their hardware first. Since RVC Next runs in its own environment, upper bounds do not hinder other projects; they prevent `pip install` from resolving to an untested combination someday. The project has been tested at both ends, both passing the test suite: torch 2.7.1 with every other dependency at its lower bound on Python 3.10, torch 2.11 with numpy 2.5, librosa 1.0 and av 19 on Python 3.12 to 3.14, and torch 2.13 and 2.14 on Python 3.12 (installed from the built wheel; the lower end with `uv pip install --resolution lowest-direct` over the direct dependencies). The development environment (Python 3.11) passes the full check."],[8236,"The public `SettingsView` excludes the access token, exposing only `access_token_configured: bool`; `env_overrides` lists names fixed by environment variables, which the UI uses to display “Set by an environment variable”."],[10834,"The PulseAudio/PipeWire monitor of each output (“Monitor of …”)"],[8359,"The repository root also contains the official license files and `manifest.json`. `manifest.json` records each file's size, SHA-256, and upstream path (which official repository path, distribution archive, or torchfcpe it came from), serving as rvc-model's own inventory. Convention: update `manifest.json` when uploading files to rvc-model; sizes, hashes, and `rvc-model` paths in `catalog.json` are generated from the manifest. No script for this step is committed in this repository; changes to `catalog.json` must remain consistent with the manifest."],[10166,"The responsibilities of these pages and that file are:"],[10867,"The result reads, for example, `Measured latency 620 ms: round trip 560 ms through the devices + conversion 60 ms (estimated 580 ms)`."],[8331,"The resulting URL is `<endpoint>/<repo>/resolve/<revision>/<sources[repository]>`. Both repositories currently use `main` as `revision`."],[10518,"The rightmost button cycles through **Light** → **Dark** → **System**, with its icon showing the current choice. More appearance options (**Colour**, **Contrast**, **Motion**) are in **Settings › Appearance**."],[7781,"The root command provides Typer's completion options:"],[11395,"The rule of the RVC package RVC Next follows: the pitch is filled in and consonant protection has no effect."],[6398,"The RVC Next server listens only on the local machine by default (127.0.0.1:7868), so only browsers on that computer can open it. This page explains how to allow other devices on the local network to connect and the protections the server applies."],[10666,"The same **Advanced** tab sets the device's **Default Format** (step 5 of VB-CABLE › Install). Some devices also show **Enable audio enhancements** (or a separate **Enhancements** tab) there; turning it off on the microphone keeps the driver's own processing from colouring the voice rvc-next converts."],[10805,"The same device panel appears in **Settings › Audio**. Both edit the same settings."],[10897,"The saved device is missing, so the system default will be used"],[7230,"The saved one"],[8674,"The scheduler thread `_schedule_loop()` checks once per second or when awakened by `notify()`: it sorts by `(-priority, created_at)` (preview conversion's `priority` is `PREVIEW_PRIORITY = 10`, so previews always jump the queue) and checks each declared resource for every queued job:"],[9733,"The script runs: `bun install --frozen-lockfile` → `bun run build` (with `--ci`, the build-only `build:only`, because CI runs type checks and tests separately) → `python -m build --outdir dist/`. Without `--ci` or `--keep-web-dist`, it deletes `rvc_next/webui/dist/` afterwards to leave the local checkout as it was. Environment variables `CI` and `KEEP_WEB_DIST` supply the defaults for these two switches."],[7774,"The selected GPU is unavailable or out of memory"],[10850,"The selected input channels are mixed to mono; output is written to the selected channels. Defaults are **Default (channel 1)** for input and **Default (channel 1+2)** for output (channel 1 for a mono device). You can also select **Channel N** or **Mix 1+2**."],[11484,"The Separate page uses RoFormer models to split audio: separate songs into vocals and accompaniment, remove reverb from vocals, and separate lead and backing vocals. Multiple steps can be chained into one preset, and any resulting stem can be sent directly to Convert."],[6409,"The server can delete and move files, read audio on the server, and open audio devices. Opening it to other computers needs no access token, but everyone who can connect has these powers: make sure the network is trusted, or add protection of your own such as a firewall, a VPN or a reverse proxy with authentication. The access token is an optional extra layer."],[6291,"The server immediately unloads all unused models so the next run starts with empty GPU memory; if the live worker fails, it unloads its own models first;"],[10388,"The server needs `pip install \"rvc-next[effects]\"`; without it the section says so and a conversion with effects is refused."],[10357,"The server needs `pip install \"rvc-next[tts]\"` (edge-tts); without it the dialog says so."],[6432,"The server notes in its log that other computers can reach it:"],[6156,"The server picks up `settings.toml` changes within about 1 second, and the Web UI refreshes too. If nothing changes, first check the server log for an “Ignored a change to … settings.toml” warning (an invalid file edit). Then check whether an environment variable overrides the same setting (`rvc-next config show` marks this). A few settings, such as the port and listening address, require a server restart."],[10993,"The server rescans devices every 2 seconds."],[6482,"The server treats a request as local only when it comes from a loopback address and its `Host` is a local name (`localhost`, `127.0.0.1`, `::1`). If the proxy and server are on the same computer and the proxy rewrites `Host` to `localhost`, all visitors are treated as local; a proxy or tunnel that keeps its public host name is not. When exposing the server to the internet, add authentication at the proxy or set an access token."],[11318,"The server validates these ranges, and the interface reads the server's definitions directly, so it cannot allow a value that the server rejects."],[10992,"The session enters **Reconnecting the device…**. The top-bar chip shows this state and turns red, and the bar at the bottom says which device went: **The output device was lost**, with its name."],[10800,"The session runs at the browser's audio rate (usually 48 or 44.1 kHz). Voice, parameters, buffering, effects, gains (from **Settings › Audio**) and recording work as with server devices; monitoring, the input meter and **Test** belong to server devices."],[6107,"The settings file, `settings.toml`"],[6110,"The settings file's full path (any file name); takes precedence over `config_dir`"],[11576,"The Settings page brings together all rvc-next options in eight tabs: General, Compute, Audio, Storage, Downloads, Training defaults, Appearance and About."],[10474,"The sheet lists jobs from all pages: jobs started in this session (with live progress) come first, followed by recent history recorded by the server, up to 30 jobs. Click **All jobs** at the top right to open the full jobs page, where you can filter by **All**, **Active** or **Finished**, or click **Clear finished**."],[10071,"The single parameter panel: identical labels, ranges, defaults, help, and order everywhere. Ranges come from `paramSpecs.ts` (server schema), order from `VOICE_FIELDS` in `paramFields.ts`; selecting a voice loads its default preset (preserving the speaker); unavailable controls explain why (index strength without an index, consonant protection without pitch guidance); dragging applies immediately in `live` mode"],[10068,"The single voice-selection entry point: search by name, show sample rate, version, and speakers, mark voices without indexes; multi-speaker voices include a speaker menu (`v-model:speaker`)"],[10077,"The single way to supply audio: drop files, select server files (`ServerPathDialog`, within `paths.browse_roots`), or select previous results (`OutputsPickerDialog`); `folders` allows a whole server folder as one input"],[9950,"The skeleton (theme, `ui/` components, motion, rule tests, API client, and socket wrapper) comes from Hanaikada, which inherited it from SD Model Hub; RVC Next adds audio components (waveforms, level meters, device menus, parameter sliders) and five feature screens. This page covers the stack, directories, shared components and unified experience contract, data flow, layout and motion rules, internationalization, tests, and steps for adding a screen."],[11245,"The small text beneath the panel explains when changes apply:"],[12103,"The source **does not include a built web interface** (`rvc_next/webui/dist` is not in the repository). Without it, `rvc-next webui` serves only the API and logs `No web UI build found`. Building the web interface requires bun:"],[12117,"The status and verification results of each asset (HuBERT, RMVPE, base models, separation models, etc.)."],[10963,"The status bar updates 10 times per second and includes:"],[9164,"The streaming engine has its own `_get_f0`/`_f0_post` to handle tensors on the device, with the same maths (see below). Training F0 is not pitch-shifted."],[9845,"The synthesizer's 18 positional configuration parameters"],[10894,"The system has not granted microphone access"],[12108,"The system PortAudio library is required. On Debian / Ubuntu:"],[11641,"The table below lists each device's ID, name, precision that will be used, and the reason if it cannot be used."],[10356,"The text is sent to Microsoft's speech service over the internet; nothing is sent until you click **Add**."],[6762,"The text to speak; `-` reads it from standard input"],[6438,"The token applies to all browsers, **including browsers on the server machine**: after setting a token, opening the Web UI locally also asks for it once. The command line does not use the API and needs no token."],[7920,"The token can be supplied in three places (`request_token()` checks in order): `Authorization: Bearer …`, the `rvc_next_token` cookie, or the `?token=` query parameter. Comparison uses `hmac.compare_digest`. Cookies support browser-initiated requests such as `<audio src>`, which cannot attach headers. `health` is public so the page shell can load without a token, read `auth_required: true`, and open `AuthDialog` to request one."],[6207,"The token is not displayed in plaintext (`config show` only reports whether it is set). Stop the server on the server computer, then set a new token or clear it:"],[7922,"The token is optional: neither a non-loopback bind nor public host names accepted through `extra_hosts` require it; `rvc-next webui` and `RvcNextServer` only log a warning. Protecting a public deployment (firewall, VPN, a reverse proxy with authentication) is left to the user."],[6284,"The top bar's **GPU memory** indicator shows used/total GPU memory and the **Loaded:** model list (or **Nothing loaded** if empty)."],[11059,"The top of the detail page has **Back**, **Download** (the voice as a zip: its `.pth`, with the speaker names written in, and its indexes, named so an import pairs them again; also `rvc-next model export`), **Show in folder** (shown only when you open the page on the server machine) and **Delete** buttons. The page has four sections:"],[11807,"The top of the experiment page shows its name, sample rate · version · folder path, with a **Delete** button on the right (unavailable while running; deleted experiments are moved to `trash/` in the data folder)."],[12114,"The torch and CUDA versions, the automatically selected compute device and precision, and each device's GPU memory and whether it is selected;"],[11776,"The Train page trains a new voice from your own recordings. Each training run is an experiment: a separate folder containing dataset settings, intermediate results from each stage, checkpoints and training curves. After training, you can try a checkpoint immediately and export it to the voice library with one click when satisfied."],[9730,"The two processes share a lifetime: when either exits, the other stops too; Ctrl+C or SIGTERM stops both. Each subprocess has its own process group, and `dev.py` forwards signals centrally, so running in the background also leaves no orphan processes."],[6464,"The user's home directory;"],[10757,"The virtual device is missing from rvc-next's menus"],[10752,"The virtual device is the system's default output, or (Voicemeeter) the VAIO strip's **B1** is on"],[10738,"The virtual device must be on the computer whose devices Live uses:"],[11019,"The voice library on disk"],[10294,"The voice library starts empty. RVC Next can download the official RVC demo voices, together with their indexes, in one click for you to try:"],[6829,"The voice library."],[7047,"The voice this preset belongs to"],[7011,"The voice this preset belongs to; omit for a global preset"],[6635,"The voice's default preset"],[6692,"The voice's own sample rate"],[9729,"The Web side runs `bun run dev --port <web-port> --strictPort`, with `RVC_NEXT_BACKEND=http://127.0.0.1:<api-port>`. `vite.config.ts` proxies `/api`, `/openapi.json` and `/ws` to that address (default `http://127.0.0.1:7868`); open `http://localhost:<web-port>` in the browser."],[9949,"The Web UI is a Vue 3 project under `rvc_next/webui/` and also a Python package: its git-ignored `dist/` build is bundled as package data in the wheel and served by the API's `SPAStaticFiles` (see API and events). It has no business logic of its own—REST is the sole data source, and socket events only keep the cache fresh."],[9936,"The Web UI is a Vue 3 project under rvcnext/webui/ and also a Python package: its git-ignored dist/ build is bundled as package data in the wheel and served by the API's SPAStaticFiles` (see API and events)."],[6136,"The web UI needs no rebuild: it derives its base path from the address of its own script. The command line supports the same with `rvc-next webui --api-prefix /voice`."],[10217,"The windowed sinc resampler in `transforms.Resample`; FCPE inference path (mel frontend, convolution-only `CFNaiveMelPE`, decoders and loader)"],[8900,"The worker announces `stopped` immediately after connecting; `_awaiting_start` makes the service ignore it until `Start` is processed, avoiding a flash back to “Stopped” in the UI during startup."],[8902,"The worker answers a meter or passthrough request with its last error kept: the meter coming back after a failed session used to clear the error before it could be read."],[8897,"The worker stays running after sessions stop (for meters and quick restarts), with models in its own process, which is why “Free GPU memory” must notify it (`P.ReleaseMemory`)."],[6911,"The zip to write"],[10435,"Theme"],[9941,"Theme and tokens"],[6180,"Then check the \"Audio devices\" line in `rvc-next doctor`, or list devices with `rvc-next live devices`. **Live is not available on this server yet.** means `sounddevice` is not installed in the server's Python environment."],[12170,"Then read the User guide as needed: start with the Interface overview, then the page you want to use."],[10711,"Then restart PulseAudio with `pulseaudio -k` (it starts again by itself)."],[10709,"Then restart the sound server (or log out and back in):"],[8316,"There are 17 assets and 37 files, totaling about 4.9 GiB. SwiftF0's 165 KB weights are not an asset: they ship inside the package (`engine/f0/data/swift_f0.pt`). Base models are split by version × sample rate instead of the planned two assets `pretrained-v1`/`pretrained-v2`: training at one rate requires downloading 4 files rather than 12 (AGENTS.md §8). Separation weights live in `pymss_weights/` instead of the planned `separation/`, also to match the original layout."],[10285,"There are also optional pitch models, needed only when you select their method: **FCPE** (about 41 MB), **CREPE** (about 85 MB) and **CREPE tiny** (about 2 MB). The `pm` and **SwiftF0** methods need no download (SwiftF0's small model comes with RVC Next)."],[10415,"There are several ways to convert many files at once:"],[8460,"There are two entry points: `[project.scripts]` in `pyproject.toml` maps `rvc-next` to `rvc_next.cli.app:main`, and `rvc_next/__main__.py` makes `python -m rvc_next` call the same `main()` (this is how `dev.py dev` starts the API)."],[10019,"There are two kinds of components:"],[10690,"There are two Linux-specific points:"],[7824,"There is one dependency: `ServicesDep = Annotated[Services, Depends(get_services)]` in `api/deps.py`, which reads services from `request.app.state`. There is no global service locator (as used by InvokeAI), so a route declares its dependencies in its signature, and tests only need to replace `app.state.services`."],[6361,"These changes are intentional (all other numerical calculations correspond line by line to the original):"],[6292,"These failed jobs **are not retried automatically**. Click **Retry** in **Jobs** yourself."],[11679,"These folders apply to every browser, those on other computers included; the command line is not limited by them."],[11392,"These frames also get no pitch, so the voice makes noise there instead of a tone, as classic RVC and Applio convert. Most voices shared online were trained this way and may sound cleaner with it."],[10712,"These remove *every* remapped source and null sink, including any you made for other purposes. Then delete the `rvc-next-virtual` block from `~/.asoundrc` and the file from **Make it permanent**."],[9145,"These two functions follow ComfyUI's `comfy/model_management.py`:"],[10695,"These two work on PipeWire and PulseAudio alike, but last only until you log out or restart the sound server. To keep them, see Make it permanent."],[11702,"These values provide the initial settings for a **New experiment**. Existing experiments are unaffected (edit each experiment's settings on its page; see Train)."],[6347,"These voices are marked **legacy**, appear in all voice pickers, and can be used directly for conversion and live voice conversion."],[8409,"These voices have no named speakers and are treated as single-speaker voices despite their usual 109-row speaker embeddings. Like the official repository, they follow the MIT license and the authors' additional terms (research use only; users bear full responsibility for generated and distributed audio)."],[10749,"They hear my real voice too, or only my real voice"],[10751,"They hear the call, music or system sounds"],[10730,"They use the system's default input: make the recording side the default in **Settings › System › Sound › Input** (Windows) or **System Settings › Sound › Input** (macOS). Linux: move the program's stream in `pavucontrol › Recording` instead (Linux, step 5)"],[10697,"This also works on PipeWire, through its PulseAudio server."],[12098,"This extra installs `torch-directml` and `onnxruntime-directml`. `torch-directml` has its own PyTorch version requirements, which can be older than the version above; install the PyTorch version it asks for, as long as it is 2.7.1 or newer. This path is experimental."],[12092,"This is AMD's own ROCm 10 build, from AMD's package index (its PyTorch releases trail the official ones, hence 2.13). The `device-all` extra installs the GPU code for every AMD GPU it supports (one `amd-torch-device-gfx…` package per GPU family), so the download is large. AMD GPUs then appear as `cuda:N`. The quotes keep shells such as zsh from reading the square brackets as a file pattern."],[10995,"This is controlled by **Settings › Audio › Reconnect a lost device automatically** (on by default). When disabled, a device disconnection stops the session immediately, and the error says which device was lost."],[6315,"This is where CPU and GPU differ most: fast GPU inference allows shorter blocks for lower latency; CPU inference is much slower and usually needs longer blocks (increasing latency). The pm pitch method is also faster than RMVPE. Longer **Context** makes the result smoother but increases computation per block; denoising adds some processing time."],[10790,"This means `sounddevice` (the Python wrapper for PortAudio) is not installed in the server's Python environment. Linux also needs the system package `libportaudio2`. See Installation."],[7739,"This page covers shared command-line conventions and common workflows. For every option of each command, see Command reference; for settings and environment variables, see Configuration and environment variables."],[11238,"This page explains each parameter and how to use presets."],[6212,"This page explains how RVC Next selects compute devices and precision, manages GPU memory, and which settings affect speed and live latency."],[6229,"This page explains how RVC Next selects compute devices and precision, manages GPU memory, and which settings affect speed and live latency. Related settings are in the `compute` group (**Settings** › **Compute** in the Web UI). For the full list, see Configuration and environment variables."],[9046,"This page explains what each engine module does and why. For rules on line-by-line comparison with the original, golden tests and behaviour changes, see Parity with the original."],[8559,"This page explains, service by service, what the code does today and why. For the overall picture of layer rules, `build_services()`, error codes, settings precedence and the event bus, see Architecture; for the asset catalog and download repositories, see Assets and model repositories; for engine internals, see Engine."],[8042,"This page gives the full picture of RVC Next: package structure and layer rules, service assembly, process responsibilities, a request's round trip from browser to engine, and shared infrastructure for errors, settings, events, the database and data directory. Each layer is covered in detail in Engine, Core services, API and events, Web UI and CLI implementation."],[7814,"This page is for maintainers adding or removing endpoints, changing security policies, or adding events: application assembly, REST conventions, uploads and media, access control, events and payloads, TypeScript generation from OpenAPI, and port selection at server startup."],[6495,"This page lists every rvc-next command and subcommand by command tree, based on each command's --help output. Help lists commands and subcommands alphabetically; this page groups them by purpose."],[12001,"This section explains how to use each page of the RVC Next web interface."],[12005,"This section explains how to use each page of the RVC Next web interface. If you have not installed or started RVC Next yet, read Quick start first. Each page also has corresponding `rvc-next` commands; see Command line."],[10159,"This section is for anyone modifying, extending or reviewing the RVC Next code: maintainers, contributors and coding agents working in this repository."],[10164,"This section is for anyone modifying, extending or reviewing the RVC Next code: maintainers, contributors and coding agents working in this repository. It explains how the code is built **today**, why each rule exists, and where it must stay consistent with the original RVC. For usage, see Quick start and the User guide; for individual commands, see Command line."],[6487,"This section is for users who can already use RVC Next and want to customize it further: reuse original RVC files, access the server from other computers, tune performance for their GPU, and troubleshoot common issues."],[12127,"This section takes you from scratch: install RVC Next, start the web interface, download the required models and complete your first conversion with a demo voice."],[11821,"This speaker's audio folder; click to select"],[8461,"This structure comes from ani2xcur-cli, carried forward through SD Model Hub."],[11